window.COURSE_CONTENT = {"classes": [{"slug": "class-1-its-just-numbers", "sourceFile": "01-class-1-its-just-numbers.md", "title": "Class 1: It's Just Numbers, Honest", "subtitle": "What AI actually is, what it isn't, and why the difference matters to you", "md": "\n**Time:** about 20 minutes of reading, plus a 15-minute exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** explain in two plain sentences what an AI model is, tell a model apart from a product like ChatGPT, list three tasks these tools are reliably good at today and three they are not, and name the economic change that matters most to a small business.\n\n## How this course teaches\n\nEvery class follows one rule, borrowed from the physicist Richard Feynman: if you can't explain something simply, you don't understand it yet. So each idea arrives as an everyday picture first. The technical word comes second, with a plain translation. Where a picture stops being accurate, we say so. And every class ends by asking you to explain the idea back in your own words, because that is the fastest way to find the part you haven't quite got.\n\n## The short version\n\nAn AI chatbot is a very large autocomplete. It has read an enormous amount of text and learned what usually comes next. It doesn't know things the way you do, so it is brilliant at drafting and terrible at being trusted without a check.\n\n## Start with something you already use\n\nYour phone guesses your next word as you type. Type \"running a bit\" and it offers \"late\". It isn't reading your mind. It has seen enough of your messages to know the usual shape of your sentences.\n\nA large language model, the thing inside ChatGPT and its rivals, is that same trick at an enormous scale. Your phone learned from your last few thousand messages. A model learned from more writing than a person could read in a hundred lifetimes. Hold that picture. Nearly everything else in this course follows from it.\n\n## What a model actually is\n\nA model is a stack of billions of numbers, arranged in a fixed pattern. That's all it is.\n\nPicture a sound desk in a recording studio, but with billions of sliders instead of forty. Building the model, which the industry calls **training**, works like this. A computer feeds in a sentence with the last word hidden, lets the desk guess the word, checks the guess, and nudges every slider a hair towards a better guess. Then it does that again, billions of times, for weeks, on warehouses of computers. When the guesses are good enough, the sliders are locked. The finished model is that locked desk: a file of numbers.\n\nWhen you type a question, your words flow across those locked sliders and out comes the most likely next word, then the next, then the next, until a full answer has been written one piece at a time.\n\n**Where the picture breaks:** a real sound desk has a person who knows what each slider does. Nobody can point at one of these billions of numbers and say what it means. The behaviour comes from all of them together, which is part of why even the companies that build these models can't fully predict what they will say.\n\n## Why confident nonsense happens\n\nThink of a student sitting an exam on a topic they never studied. They have read plenty of top-mark essays, so they know exactly what a good answer sounds like: the confident tone, the structure, a reference or two. So they write one. It reads beautifully. Some of it is invented.\n\nThat is what a model does when it hasn't seen reliable information about your exact question. It is built to produce the most plausible next words, and plausible is not the same as true. So it produces the shape of an answer: a confident sentence, a realistic-looking reference, a number in roughly the right range.\n\nThe industry calls this a **hallucination**. Plain English is better: the model made it up. It doesn't know it made it up, because it has no separate sense of \"true\" to check against. This one fact explains most AI horror stories. It is why every later class ends with a person checking the names, numbers, dates and quotes before anything leaves the building.\n\n## Tokens: how the model reads\n\nModels don't read whole words. They read in small chunks called **tokens**, roughly three-quarters of an English word each. \"Unbelievable\" might be three tokens: \"un\", \"believ\", \"able\".\n\nYou will never need to count them. You need the idea because tokens are how the meter runs. Prices, speed and memory limits are all measured in tokens, the way your power bill is measured in kilowatt-hours.\n\n## How much it can hold in mind at once\n\nPicture the model working at a desk. Everything it is considering right now has to fit on the desk: your question, any document you pasted, and the conversation so far. The size of that desk is called the **context window**.\n\nWhen the desk fills up, the oldest papers slide off the far edge and the model can no longer see them. That's why a very long chat starts forgetting what you agreed at the beginning, and why Class 4 says to start a fresh chat for each new task.\n\n## The engine and the car\n\nThe model is the engine. ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and Copilot are cars. Each is a software product built around an engine, with its own dashboard, its own price list, its own terms and conditions, and a company behind it trying to make money.\n\nTwo cars can share an engine. A car maker can also swap the engine under the bonnet without much fuss. The practical lesson is that you are a car buyer, not an engineer. Cars can be test-driven, compared, cancelled and negotiated over. Judge the car by what it does with your work, and treat the engine as a detail.\n\n## What these tools are good at today\n\n- Drafting and rewriting almost any kind of text, from emails to policy documents.\n- Summarising long material and pulling structure out of messy notes.\n- Translating jargon into plain language, and back again.\n- First drafts of spreadsheet formulas, checklists and small bits of code.\n- Brainstorming, when you need fifteen ideas and have three.\n\nNotice what these have in common. In every case, a rough first draft saves time and a person checks the result. That is the sweet spot, and it's a big one.\n\n## What they are bad at today\n\n- **Facts you haven't given them.** Unless you supply the facts, or the product does a live web search you can inspect, assume the bluffing student.\n- **Long sums.** A model predicts the next word, it doesn't calculate. For maths, ask it to write a spreadsheet formula, then check the formula.\n- **Your business.** It knows nothing about your clients, prices or habits unless you told it in this conversation or in a saved setting.\n- **Saying \"I don't know\".** These tools rarely admit uncertainty unless they were tuned to. A confident tone tells you nothing about accuracy.\n\n## The quiet change that matters most\n\nThink about suits. A tailored suit used to cost a fortune because a skilled person spent days on it, so most people bought off the rack and lived with sleeves that were a bit long.\n\nSoftware was the same. In 1995, custom software meant developers, servers, licences and boxes of disks. So it was built once and sold to thousands of businesses, and a ten-person company bent its habits around whatever came off the rack.\n\nTwo things have changed. First, making one more copy of software, or adding one more user, now costs almost nothing. Economists call that the **marginal cost**, and for software it's close to zero. Second, AI tools now write much of the routine code. Together, that means software tailored to your one business, which used to be a six-figure conversation, can sometimes be an afternoon. Class 5 is entirely about this, because it's the change that will actually show up in your budget.\n\n## Cloud or local, in thirty seconds\n\nMost AI products run in the **cloud**. That means on the vendor's computers, with your text travelling to them over the internet. It's like hiring a car: convenient, always serviced, but it isn't yours, and they know where you drove.\n\nSome models are published so you can run them on your own computer. That's called running **locally**. It's like owning the ute: nobody else sees where it goes, but you do the servicing. Class 3 turns this into a decision you can make about your own data.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"AI understands language.\"** It produces language that reads as if understanding sits behind it, because it learned from text written by people who did understand.\n\nPicture a parrot that has listened to ten thousand accountants. It can produce a very convincing sentence about tax. It isn't consulting any idea of tax. It is reproducing the shape tax explanations usually take. That is why these tools are brilliant on the common case and confidently wrong on the unusual one.\n\n**Where the picture breaks:** a parrot repeats phrases it heard. A model combines patterns into sentences nobody has written before, which is why it is genuinely useful. But the lesson holds. Treat every output as fluent pattern-matching, not understanding, and you have the one habit that prevents most AI disasters in business.\n\n## Your exercise: two products, one task\n\n1. Write one real work task as a request. For example: draft a polite reminder email to a customer who is 30 days overdue on an invoice of $2,300.\n2. Paste the identical request into two different products. Free accounts are fine. Nothing sensitive goes in.\n3. Compare the two answers for length, tone and confidence, and look for any invented detail, such as a made-up interest rate or deadline. Note which one you would send with less editing.\n4. Add one firm instruction, such as \"maximum 80 words, no apologies, mention that work continues on schedule\", and run both again. Note how much the answers improve.\n5. Write one sentence answering this: was the difference between the two products bigger, or the difference made by your instruction? Most people find the instruction wins. That finding is Class 4 in miniature.\n\n## Explain it back\n\nImagine a colleague who has never used one of these tools asks you, \"So what is AI, actually?\" Write your answer in three sentences, with no technical words. Then compare it with \"The short version\" at the top of this class.\n\nIf you found yourself reaching for \"it thinks\" or \"it knows\", reread \"What a model actually is\". If you couldn't say why it makes things up, reread the bluffing student. The part you stumble on is the part you haven't got yet. That stumble is useful, not embarrassing.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can explain what a model is without saying \"it thinks\".\n- I can name the product I use most and say which company's engine likely sits under it.\n- I can list one task I will try this week and one task I will keep fully human for now.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Model:** the trained file of numbers that does the pattern-matching. The engine.\n- **Product or app:** the software you actually use, built around a model, such as ChatGPT or Copilot. The car.\n- **Training:** the one-off, expensive process of setting the model's numbers by showing it huge amounts of text. It happens at the vendor, long before you type anything.\n- **Token:** a small chunk of text, about three-quarters of a word. The unit prices and limits are measured in.\n- **Prompt:** the text you type to the model.\n- **System prompt:** standing instructions placed before your prompt that shape tone, format and rules. Class 4 and Document 1 cover writing your own.\n- **Context window:** how much text the model can consider at once. The size of its desk.\n- **Hallucination:** a confident, plausible, wrong answer. The model made it up.\n- **Weights:** another word for the model's numbers. \"Open weights\" means the numbers are published, so anyone can run the model on their own hardware.\n- **Local model:** a model running on your own computer or office server instead of a vendor's cloud."}, {"slug": "class-2-selling-the-sizzle", "sourceFile": "02-class-2-selling-the-sizzle.md", "title": "Class 2: Selling the Sizzle", "subtitle": "How to read AI marketing without losing your lunch or your budget", "md": "\n**Time:** about 25 minutes of reading, plus a 15-minute exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** apply a two-line field test to any AI claim, recognise five marketing tells by name, run a vendor demo like a buyer, and use ten questions that separate capability from brochure.\n\n## The short version\n\nAI marketing sells you a future. Your job is to buy only the present. If a claim describes what the software does today, with your data, on your screen, you can test it. If it asks you to imagine what it will do, it's a brochure.\n\n## You already know how to do this\n\nYou've read real estate listings. You know \"cosy\" means small, \"character-filled\" means old, and \"close to transport\" means a train line at the back fence. Nobody taught you that in a class. You learned it by seeing enough listings and enough houses.\n\nAI marketing has its own version of \"cosy\", and this class is the translation guide. The skill underneath is the same one you already use on real estate: notice when a word is doing a job other than describing the thing.\n\n## The field test, in two lines\n\nIf a claim needs you to imagine a future that hasn't arrived yet, it's marketing. If a claim describes what the software does today, on your screen, with your data, it's probably real.\n\nThat's the whole tool. \"Our agents will handle your entire customer service operation\" is the first kind. \"Paste in this 40-page contract and it produces a clause-by-clause summary in about a minute\" is the second kind. You could check the second one yourself before lunch.\n\nEverything else in this class is practice at using those two lines under pressure, in a meeting, with a confident person across the table.\n\n## Why AI marketing is unusually thick\n\nThere are three reasons, all commercial and none mysterious.\n\n- **They've spent a fortune.** The vendors are in a land grab and have spent billions on datacentres. The pitch has to justify the spend.\n- **There's a real story to stretch.** The technology genuinely improved fast between 2022 and 2024, which handed marketers something true to inflate.\n- **Quality is uneven.** These tools are excellent at some tasks and poor at others. A demo can show off the easy 20 percent of the work and let you assume the hard 80 percent goes the same way.\n\nKnowing why the sizzle exists makes it easier to hear past it, without becoming so cynical that you miss the genuinely useful product underneath.\n\n## Demos are theatre. Pilots are evidence.\n\nA vendor demo is an open home. The furniture is staged, the bread is baking, and nobody opens the cupboard under the sink. The prompts are chosen in advance, the tasks are forgiving, and the retries happen off screen.\n\nYou wouldn't buy a house without a building inspection. The buyer's version for software is a **pilot test**. You bring the data and the task, using your own material, in a session you control, and you agree in writing beforehand what counts as success.\n\nIf a vendor refuses a pilot on your data, treat the refusal as the answer. It's the most informative sentence a salesperson can say.\n\n## Five case studies, and what each one teaches\n\n**Deloitte and the refund (October 2025).** Deloitte Australia delivered a report to the federal Department of Home Affairs, reportedly worth about $440,000. Reviewers found it contained AI-generated errors, including invented references and made-up quotations attributed to real academics. Deloitte admitted AI tools had been used contrary to its own contract terms, and refunded part of the payment.\n\nThe lesson isn't \"consultants are careless\". It's that a global firm with every resource still shipped confident nonsense, the bluffing student from Class 1. A person who checked the citations caught it. Checking is the product you're actually paying for.\n\n**Klarna's 700 agents (claimed 2024, walked back 2025).** The Swedish fintech announced its AI assistant was doing the work of 700 full-time customer-service agents. By May 2025, its chief executive was publicly admitting the push had gone too far on quality, and human staff were being hired back.\n\nThe lesson: \"handles the volume of 700 agents\" is a marketing figure with no published method. Cost cutting advertised as AI transformation often turns out to be plain cost cutting. Analysts have a name for dressing ordinary cuts in AI language: **AI washing**. Think of the washing powder that is \"new and improved\" every year with the same ingredients.\n\n**Air Canada's chatbot (tribunal decision, February 2024).** A customer asked the airline's website chatbot about bereavement fares and got wrong information. The airline then argued the chatbot was a separate legal entity, responsible for its own actions. The tribunal rejected that argument and ordered the airline to pay.\n\nThe lesson belongs on your office wall: whatever your chatbot tells a customer, your business owns it. A chatbot is a staff member you can't fire for what it said, because legally it was you talking.\n\n**The regulators have a word for this (March 2024, United States).** The Securities and Exchange Commission fined two investment advisers, Delphia and Global Predictions, US$225,000 and US$175,000 for claiming AI capabilities they didn't have. Australian regulators have signalled the same scrutiny under existing consumer law.\n\nThe lesson: exaggerated AI claims are becoming something regulators punish. You can fairly ask a vendor what would happen to their claims if a regulator tested them, and watch the face.\n\n**McDonald's drive-through (trial ended June 2024).** The chain ended a trial of automated order-taking after viral videos showed the system adding bacon to ice cream and hundreds of dollars of nuggets to ordinary orders.\n\nThe lesson: when public-facing AI fails, someone films it, and the damage lands on your brand, not the vendor's.\n\n## The sizzle vocabulary, translated\n\nLike \"cosy\" and \"character-filled\", these words describe the seller's hopes more than the product.\n\n- **Agentic:** the software tries to do several steps in a row on its own, instead of answering one question. How reliable that is varies wildly by task, so ask to see your task.\n- **PhD-level or expert-level:** a good score on one narrow standard test, called a benchmark. It is a test result, not a job qualification. A learner driver can ace the written test.\n- **10x productivity:** a multiple with no starting point. Ask: ten times what, measured how, by whom?\n- **Enterprise-grade:** a pricing tier, not a quality guarantee. It describes the size of the contract, not the quality of the work.\n- **Powered by AI:** sometimes means one summary button bolted onto an ordinary product. Ask which feature, exactly.\n- **Seamlessly integrates:** ask which system, which version, and for a customer you can ring who uses that connection.\n\n## Listen to the grammar\n\nBrochures speak in the future tense. \"Will\", \"soon\", \"on our roadmap\" and \"imagine a world where\" are the sound of a listing describing the house after the renovation you'd be paying for. \"Roadmap\" is vendor language for features that don't exist yet.\n\nAsk the vendor to repeat every \"will\" sentence as a present-tense sentence about today's product. Whatever survives is the product you can buy. The rest is a hope with a price tag.\n\n## Take these ten questions to your next demo\n\n1. Show me my data in your tool, not your sample data.\n2. What exactly happens to data I upload, and where is it stored?\n3. Do you train on my data, and which pricing tier changes that answer?\n4. How does this tool typically go wrong, and who catches it when it does?\n5. Which connection to my other systems works today, and which is on the roadmap?\n6. Name two customers my size, in my industry, that I can call.\n7. What does your contract say about who is responsible for wrong output?\n8. What is the total first-year cost, including seats, minimums and setup work?\n9. What happens to my data and my workflows if I cancel?\n10. What did you claim last year that turned out to be wrong?\n\nQuestion ten is the character test. The answers are reliably revealing, and a vendor who answers it well has earned the rest of the meeting.\n\n## Point the test inward\n\nThe same habit works inside your own business. When a staff member says AI will save ten hours a week, ask which task, measured how, checked when. When a board paper says \"we are adopting AI\", replace that sentence with one claim about what the software does today, and see whether it still stands. Class 6 turns this habit into paperwork your team will actually use.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"If a big, reputable company says it, the capability must be real.\"** Reputation and accuracy are different products.\n\nDeloitte is one of the most established professional-services brands on earth, and still refunded part of a government report over invented citations. Klarna's chief executive personally announced the 700-agent figure and personally walked it back. Air Canada's lawyers argued, on the record, that a chatbot was a separate legal entity, and lost.\n\nNone of these organisations is stupid. Each repeated confident AI output, or an inflated AI story, because it suited a narrative, a share price or a sales cycle. A claim earns trust from the evidence behind it, never from the size of the logo beside it.\n\n## Your exercise: roast one brochure\n\n1. Find one piece of real AI marketing: a vendor email, a product page, a flyer or a sponsored post.\n2. Underline every sentence that describes something you could check today. Circle every sentence that asks you to imagine the future.\n3. Rewrite your three most circled sentences as honest present-tense claims.\n4. Write down the three hardest questions from the ten above that this material carefully avoids answering.\n5. Decide, in one sentence: would this vendor survive a pilot test on your data?\n\nNotice that your honest rewrites are usually still sellable. Real capability doesn't need the sizzle, which is exactly why the sizzle is a warning sign.\n\n## Explain it back\n\nA colleague forwards you a vendor email that says \"our AI agents will transform your business\". In three sentences, with no technical words, explain to them how to tell whether it's worth a meeting. Then compare your answer with \"The short version\" and \"The field test, in two lines\".\n\nIf you couldn't say what a pilot test is in one breath, reread \"Demos are theatre\". If you weren't sure why big names get it wrong, reread the case studies.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can state the field test from memory in two lines.\n- I can name two marketing tells I have personally heard in the last month.\n- I have the ten questions saved somewhere I will actually find them before the next demo.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **AI washing:** describing a product, process or layoff as AI-driven when the AI part is minimal, unproven or absent. Regulators now treat it as an offence category.\n- **Benchmark:** a standard test used to compare models. Useful for vendors, narrow for buyers.\n- **Pilot test:** a short, agreed, measurable trial on your own data and tasks before you buy. The building inspection.\n- **Agentic AI:** software that attempts several steps on its own instead of answering one question. Treat every agentic claim as unproven until you've seen it work on your own workflow.\n- **Roadmap:** vendor language for features that don't exist yet. Never pay for a roadmap.\n- **Failure mode:** the typical way a tool goes wrong. A mature vendor can describe theirs plainly.\n- **Total cost of ownership:** seats plus minimums plus setup plus training plus the staff time spent checking output. The number that belongs in your budget."}, {"slug": "class-3-your-data-their-servers", "sourceFile": "03-class-3-your-data-their-servers.md", "title": "Class 3: Your Data, Their Servers", "subtitle": "Cloud versus local AI, what \"training on your data\" means, and what Australian privacy law expects of you", "md": "\n**Time:** about 25 minutes of reading, plus a 10-minute settings exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** trace where your text goes when you type it, choose between cloud, local and contracted business tiers for a given task, switch off training on your data on each major platform, and run a three-question drill before pasting anything sensitive.\n\n## The short version\n\nWhen you type into a chatbot, you are sending your words to someone else's computer, and they keep a copy. Paid business accounts come with a contract about what they may do with that copy; free accounts mostly don't. So the rule is simple: work data goes into work accounts, and anything truly sensitive either stays out or stays on your own machine.\n\n## Follow one message on its journey\n\nPicture typing into a chatbot as posting a letter to a company overseas.\n\nYou write a customer's details into the chat and press enter. The letter travels over the internet to the vendor's computers, usually in the United States. Their model reads it, along with everything earlier in the conversation, and posts a reply back. Along the way, three things usually happen to your letter:\n\n- **It's filed.** A copy goes into your chat history, so you can scroll back to it.\n- **It's logged.** A copy sits in the vendor's own records, for running and protecting the service.\n- **It may be read.** In some circumstances, vendor staff or contractors read a sample of conversations, or ones flagged by their safety systems.\n\nWhen you later delete the chat, you shred your own copy and ask the vendor to shred theirs, which they do on their own timetable. You can't un-post the letter.\n\nHold that journey in mind. Every rule in this class is a decision about one leg of it.\n\n## Cloud AI, honestly\n\n**Cloud AI** means the model runs on the vendor's computers. Class 1 compared it to a hire car.\n\nWhat you get: nothing to install or maintain, the biggest and most capable models (which live in warehouses of computers, not laptops), and automatic updates.\n\nWhat you give up: control over exactly where your data sits, who at the vendor could technically see it, and what happens to it under the laws of the vendor's home country. For a small Australian business the practical risk of that last point is low. It isn't zero, though, and it isn't yours to wave away on your customers' behalf, so it belongs in the decision rather than in a blind spot.\n\n## Local AI, honestly\n\n**Local AI** means the model runs on your own computer or office server. That's the ute you own.\n\nWhat you get: your data never leaves the building, it works with no internet, and nobody's terms and conditions sit between you and your files.\n\nWhat you give up: you do the servicing. Someone has to choose the model, install updates and notice if the quality drops. And the models small enough to run on ordinary computers are less capable than the biggest cloud ones. They trail on hard reasoning, but they're perfectly good at drafting, summarising and searching your own documents.\n\n## What local needs in 2026\n\nA model is a stack of numbers, and each number is called a **parameter**. More parameters usually means a more capable model, and also a bigger file that needs more memory to run. Think of it as a bigger book needing a bigger desk to lay it open on.\n\nRough rules of thumb:\n\n- **Small models,** around 7 to 9 billion parameters, run acceptably on a computer with 16GB of memory. That includes an ordinary modern laptop and any Apple Silicon Mac with 16GB. Examples are Meta's Llama family and similar-sized Qwen, Mistral and Gemma models. These are **open-weights** models, meaning their numbers are published so anyone can run them.\n- **Mid-size models,** 12 to 32 billion parameters, want 24 to 32GB of memory, or a graphics card with 12GB or more of its own memory.\n\nTwo free programs make this close to point-and-click: **Ollama** and **LM Studio**. Install one, click a model name, and you have a private chat window. That's genuinely the whole setup.\n\n## The hybrid pattern we recommend\n\nDon't treat cloud versus local as either-or. Treat it as a sorting rule, like deciding what goes in the general bin, the recycling and the locked shredding bin.\n\n- **Free or cheap consumer accounts:** low-sensitivity work. Marketing copy, brainstorming, summarising public documents, translating jargon.\n- **Contracted business accounts,** such as ChatGPT Business, Claude Team, Microsoft 365 Copilot or Google Workspace with Gemini: ordinary work data. These tiers carry contract promises not to train on your content, and they come with admin controls.\n- **Local models, or a contracted tool with a signed agreement:** the genuinely sensitive material. Anything under a non-disclosure agreement, health information, financials before they're announced, employee disputes, raw client lists.\n\nMost ten-person businesses land comfortably on the middle row, plus one local option for the sharp edges.\n\n## \"Training on your data\", precisely\n\nPicture a teacher who marks thousands of essays every year. Reading all those essays slowly changes how the teacher thinks good writing looks, and that shapes next year's teaching. Your essay played a tiny part. But the teacher can't hand your essay to another student, because it isn't kept in a filing cabinet for anyone to open. It just nudged their habits.\n\n**Training on your data** is like that. Your prompts and documents may be used to adjust the numbers inside a future version of a model that everyone uses. It does not mean your client list becomes a database another customer can search. A model repeating one small business's documents word for word is very unlikely.\n\nThe everyday risks are quieter, and more certain:\n\n- **Retention:** your data sitting in the vendor's records for months or years. That's the essay sitting in the staffroom filing cabinet.\n- **Human review:** vendor staff reading sampled or flagged conversations.\n- **Breaches:** your data being exposed if the vendor is hacked.\n\nTraining is the headline. Retention and review are the daily reality.\n\n## The opt-out map, consumer accounts\n\nSettings move, so treat this as the shape of things and re-check every quarter. The exact click-paths are in Document 5.\n\n- **ChatGPT:** Free and Plus conversations may be used to improve models unless you opt out in Settings, Data controls. Pro is excluded. Business and Enterprise are excluded by contract.\n- **Claude:** consumer chats are kept about 30 days by default, and since the August 2025 terms change, training on your chats is opt-in. If you opt in, retention stretches to five years, so leave it off at work. Team and Enterprise content is not used for training.\n- **Gemini:** consumer prompts feed product improvement only if Gemini Apps Activity is switched on. Find it and switch it off. Workspace business prompts are not used to train models outside your organisation by default.\n- **Copilot:** consumer Copilot chats may be used to improve Microsoft's services unless you opt out in privacy settings. Anything used while signed in with a work or school account sits under commercial data protection and isn't used to train foundation models.\n- **Local tools:** nothing leaves your machine, so there's nothing to opt out of.\n\n## The cheapest security upgrade in this course\n\nMove staff off free consumer accounts and onto a business tier.\n\nThink of the difference between letting a stranger mind your shop and hiring a staff member with a signed contract. The work might be identical. What changes is the paperwork: what they may do with what they see, how long they keep it, and who's responsible if it goes wrong.\n\nThat's what a business tier buys: no training on your business content, a data processing agreement, admin controls, and a defined time your data is kept. On consumer accounts those promises mostly don't exist. For roughly the price of a lunch per person per month, the scariest paragraph in this class stops applying to you.\n\n## What a data processing agreement is\n\nThink of the register a business keeps of who holds keys to the building: who has which key, which doors it opens, and when they hand it back.\n\nA **data processing agreement**, or DPA, is that register for your customers' personal information. It's the part of the contract that says what the vendor may do with personal information you give it: what for, how it's secured, how fast they tell you about a breach, deleting it when asked, and the rules for any subcontractors.\n\nBusiness and enterprise tiers come with a DPA automatically. Consumer accounts offer no DPA and nothing equivalent. If your business handles personal information and staff use AI tools for work, you need the tool's business tier with its DPA, or you need to keep sensitive data out of the tool. There's no third option that survives scrutiny.\n\n## The Australian Privacy Act, briefly and practically\n\n**Who's covered:** organisations with annual turnover above $3 million, plus many smaller ones regardless of size, including health service providers, businesses that trade in personal information, and Commonwealth contractors. A small-business exemption still exists, but it has been flagged for removal in future reforms, so don't build your plans on it.\n\nThe Act is built from 13 rules called the Australian Privacy Principles, or APPs. Four points matter for AI tools:\n\n1. **APP 8, sending data overseas.** When staff paste personal information into a US-hosted chatbot, you generally stay responsible for how it's handled overseas. It's like a builder who stays responsible for a subcontractor's work. Contract protections are your friend here.\n2. **APP 11, security.** You must take reasonable steps to protect personal information. A written AI policy plus business-tier accounts are exactly such steps.\n3. **Automated decisions.** From 10 December 2026, privacy policies must describe significant automated decision-making: decisions about people that software, including AI, makes or substantially shapes.\n4. **Serious invasions and breaches.** A legal right to sue over serious invasions of privacy has applied since June 2025. And if a vendor suffers a breach involving your customers' personal information that risks serious harm, the Notifiable Data Breaches scheme may require you to tell the regulator (the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner, or OAIC) and the affected people.\n\nNone of this needs a lawyer for basic compliance. All of it needs one page of policy, which Class 6 and Documents 3 and 5 hand you.\n\n## The decision drill\n\nBefore you paste anything, picture writing it on a postcard: something that passes through several hands on the way. Then ask three questions.\n\n1. Does this text contain personal information, secrets, or anything under a non-disclosure agreement?\n2. If yes, is the tool on a business tier with a DPA, or running locally?\n3. If neither, rewrite the text to remove the sensitive parts (initials instead of names, \"a client\" instead of the client's name), or move to the local tool.\n\nRepeat until it's automatic. This drill is 90 percent of AI data hygiene.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"If I delete the chat, the data is gone.\"** Deleting removes your copy of the letter, not necessarily theirs.\n\nConsumer conversations usually stay in the vendor's records for a while after you delete them, and longer if they were picked for training or flagged for review. Deleting does nothing about copies already in backups. On business tiers, how long data is kept is set by contract, and deletion requests are honoured within a stated time. That's part of what you pay for.\n\nTreat anything you paste into a consumer tool as permanent, because you can't reliably make it impermanent afterwards. The decision about what not to paste happens before the paste.\n\n## Your exercise: settings safari\n\n1. Open the AI account you use most, on the device you use most.\n2. Find three things and write down the click-path, because menus move: the data or privacy settings page, the switch that controls whether conversations are used for training or \"product improvement\", and the place to view or delete your history.\n3. Turn the training or improvement switch off. Read what the screen says will change.\n4. Find the temporary or no-save chat mode, if the tool has one, and run one harmless prompt through it.\n5. If this is a work account, write one question for your admin, for example: \"Are we on a business tier where our content is excluded from training?\" Ask it next week.\n\nThe finding is usually the same: the controls exist, and nobody advertises them.\n\n## Explain it back\n\nYour newest staff member asks: \"Is it okay if I paste a customer's complaint email into ChatGPT to help me reply?\" In three sentences, with no jargon, explain what happens to that email and what they should do. Then compare your answer with \"The short version\" and the decision drill.\n\nIf you weren't sure where the email ends up, reread the letter's journey. If you mixed up training and retention, reread the teacher and the filing cabinet.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can say where my text goes when I type it into my usual tool.\n- I know which tier my account is on and whether training is off.\n- I can run the decision drill out loud without reading it.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Cloud AI:** the model runs on the vendor's computers and your data travels to them. The hire car.\n- **Local AI:** the model runs on your hardware and your data stays with you. The ute you own.\n- **Open weights:** a model whose numbers are published, so anyone can download and run it. Often free for businesses below a very large size.\n- **Parameter:** one of the numbers in a model. \"8B parameters\" means eight billion numbers. More usually means more capable, and more hardware.\n- **Ollama and LM Studio:** free programs that make running a local model close to point-and-click.\n- **Training on your data:** using your text to adjust the numbers in a future model. The teacher's habits, not a filing cabinet.\n- **Retention:** how long the vendor keeps your data, even if it never trains on it. The quieter, more certain risk.\n- **DPA:** data processing agreement, the contract saying what a vendor may do with your customers' personal information. The key register.\n- **APP:** Australian Privacy Principle, one of the 13 rules in the Privacy Act. APP 8 covers sending data overseas, APP 11 covers security.\n- **Notifiable Data Breaches scheme:** the requirement to tell the OAIC and affected people about breaches likely to cause serious harm, including breaches at your vendor.\n- **ADM:** automated decision-making, decisions about people made or substantially shaped by software. Disclosure duties begin 10 December 2026."}, {"slug": "class-4-talking-to-the-software", "sourceFile": "04-class-4-talking-to-the-software.md", "title": "Class 4: Talking to the Software", "subtitle": "Prompting without the prompt-guru nonsense: context, system prompts, and being specific", "md": "\n**Time:** about 20 minutes of reading, plus a 20-minute setup exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** write a request in four parts that gets a usable first draft, explain why long conversations degrade, install a standing system prompt on your platform of choice, and fix the three common failure modes without starting over.\n\n## The short version\n\nTalk to an AI tool the way you'd brief a capable temp on their first day: say who they're working for, what you want, give them the real material, and set the limits. Put the things you'd otherwise repeat every day into a standing briefing. Then read what comes back and correct it, the way you'd correct a new starter.\n\n## Kill the magic thinking first\n\nThere's a whole industry selling \"prompt engineering\" secrets, and most of it is superstition. There are no magic words. You don't need to say please, and you don't need to tell it that it's a world-class expert.\n\nHere's why. From Class 1, a model is a pattern-matcher working on whatever is on its desk right now. So better results come from better desk contents: clearer instructions, the right material, fewer distractions. That's the whole secret. It's good news, because it means anyone who can write a clear work email can learn this in an evening.\n\n## The four-part request: a job sheet\n\nThink of the job sheet a tradie hands an apprentice. It says who the job is for, what to do, what materials to use, and the standards to meet. Leave any of that off and the apprentice guesses.\n\nA good request to an AI tool has the same four parts:\n\n1. **Role.** Who the tool is working for. \"You're helping a plumbing business in Geelong.\"\n2. **Task.** What you want made. \"Draft a quote cover email.\"\n3. **Material.** The real ingredients, pasted in or attached: the job details, the prices, the customer's original message.\n4. **Constraints.** The limits: length, tone, format, what to avoid, and what to do if something's unclear.\n\nMost disappointing results are missing parts three and four. People ask for the finished product without handing over the ingredients or the standards, then blame the tool for guessing. Here's the difference in one pair:\n\n- **Weak:** \"Write a professional email about the delay.\"\n- **Strong:** \"Write a 100-word email to Karen at Bayside Dental apologising that the hot-water unit install moves from Thursday to Tuesday the 21st, offering a $150 discount on the labour, and confirming the new date. Warm, plain English, no corporate phrases.\"\n\nThe weak version leaves a thousand plausible directions open, and the model picks one. The strong version closes 999 of them. Every limit you add is an edit you won't have to make later.\n\n## The desk, properly explained\n\nClass 1 introduced the model's desk, the **context window**. Here's why it matters for everyday use.\n\nEverything shares the same desk: your standing briefing, any documents you pasted, the whole conversation so far, and the reply being written. The desk is a fixed size, measured in tokens. When it overflows, the oldest papers fall off the far edge, and the model genuinely can no longer see the start of the conversation.\n\nThree practical consequences:\n\n- **Long chats get worse near the end.** Start a new chat for a new task.\n- **Huge documents get skimmed.** A 300-page PDF may be answered from a fraction of its pages, so ask pointed questions about specific sections.\n- **\"Remember what I said at the start\" is unreliable** in a long conversation. Standing instructions, in the next section, are what fix that.\n\n## The system prompt: your standing briefing\n\nPicture the laminated card you'd leave on a temp's desk: who we are, who our customers are, how we write, and the things we never do. They read it every morning without being asked.\n\nA **system prompt** is that card. Depending on the product it's called custom instructions, personal context, or project instructions. It's text the product quietly places in front of every message you send. Put the rules you're tired of repeating there: who your business is, who you write to, your tone, your usual formats, and your bright lines, such as \"never invent prices or dates\".\n\nDocument 1 in your pack has three complete templates, for retail, professional services and trades businesses, plus the exact menu path to paste them into ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot and local tools. Installing one is the most valuable twenty minutes in this course.\n\n## Three ways to show it your documents\n\nThink about how you'd give a temp information:\n\n- **Pasting into the chat** is handing them one sheet of paper. It's on the desk straight away, all of it, and it's gone when the chat ends. Best for the one important document, right now.\n- **Uploading a file to the chat** is handing them a folder. The product converts your PDF, Word file, spreadsheet or photo into text and puts it on the desk for this task. Clean digital documents convert well. Tables, diagrams and photos of pages convert imperfectly.\n- **Adding files to a project** (also called a Gem, notebook or knowledge base) is putting them in the office library. You store the material once, and every conversation in that space can use it. For each question, the product fetches the relevant pages, like a librarian bringing you the right chapter. Best for standing reference: policies, price lists, templates.\n\nDocument 2 covers the details, including the honest limits of what these tools can do with files. Rule of thumb: the library for standing material, a pasted page for today's facts.\n\n## Correcting the draft is the real skill\n\nThe first answer is a draft, not a finished job. You wouldn't expect a new starter's first attempt to go straight to a client either.\n\nThe working rhythm is: ask, read it critically, correct it (\"that's too formal, and you invented a deadline, take it out\"), narrow it down, and run it again. Two or three rounds of correction reliably beat one perfect attempt, because you're steering with feedback, not programming a machine.\n\nOne phrase earns its place in your toolkit: \"Before answering, list what information you'd need that I haven't given you.\" It turns a guesser into a question-asker, and questions are cheaper than corrections.\n\n## Three common problems and their fixes\n\n- **Vague, generic output.** You under-briefed it. Add limits and paste real material.\n- **Wrong tone.** Fix it once in the standing briefing, not message by message.\n- **Invented facts.** The model hit a gap and filled it with something plausible, the bluffing student from Class 1. Give it the facts, then make it an open-book exam: tell it to answer only from the material you provided, and to say \"not in the material provided\" otherwise. That's called a **grounding instruction**. Then check every name, number, date and quote anyway. Grounding reduces invention. It doesn't eliminate it, so checking is never optional.\n\n## The extras around the engine\n\nProducts bolt useful features onto the model. They differ by vendor because, in Class 1's terms, they're features of the car, not the engine.\n\n- **Live web search** fixes the \"knows nothing recent\" problem. It reads the web imperfectly, much like you do when skimming, so check anything that matters.\n- **Editable document panes,** called Canvas in ChatGPT and Artifacts in Claude, let you revise a document in place instead of regenerating the whole answer.\n- **Built into your office software:** Copilot drafts inside Word and Outlook, and Gemini inside Gmail and Docs. That matters if your business already lives in one of those suites.\n\nFor basic writing tasks, all four major products are close enough. Let the software you already use, and the data protection on your account tier, decide, not the test scores in the marketing.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"Prompting is a new profession and you need a course in magic words.\"** Prompt libraries full of \"act as a world-class expert with 20 years of experience\" are mostly theatre. Modern models respond to plain, specific instructions at least as well as to elaborate role-play.\n\nWhat changes the output is unglamorous: put the right material in front of the model, say precisely what you want and what to avoid, correct it when it misses, and save your standing instructions so you aren't retyping them.\n\nA plumber who can describe a job clearly to an apprentice can prompt well, because it's the same skill: knowing the job, setting the standard, checking the work. What's genuinely worth learning is the furniture around the engine, such as projects, files, search and memory. That's different in each product, which is why your document pack tells you where each button lives.\n\n## Your exercise: build your standing setup\n\n1. Draft a five-line system prompt for your business with this skeleton: who we are, who we write to, tone rules, format rules, and one \"never\" rule such as \"never invent prices or dates\". Start from the matching template in Document 1.\n2. Paste it into the right place on your platform. Document 9 has the click-paths. If your work tool is managed by an admin, send them the template instead and count that as done.\n3. Start a new chat and give the tool one real document plus one real task, such as \"summarise this into five bullets for a customer who has never seen it\". Nothing sensitive, or remove the sensitive parts first.\n4. Run one round of correction using the fixes above. Note what changed.\n5. Write down the one recurring task this setup will handle next week.\n\nThat last sentence is the point of the exercise. The time saved doesn't come from one great prompt. It comes from a standing setup that makes the tool useful on the tenth ordinary Tuesday.\n\n## Explain it back\n\nA colleague says, \"I tried ChatGPT and it gave me useless generic rubbish.\" In three sentences, with no jargon, explain what they probably left out and how to fix it. Then compare your answer with \"The short version\" and the job sheet.\n\nIf you weren't sure why long chats go wrong, reread the desk. If you couldn't explain the difference between a one-off message and a standing briefing, reread the laminated card.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- My four-part request skeleton is written somewhere I can copy it.\n- My system prompt is installed, or queued with my admin.\n- I know which problem I meet most often and which fix matches it.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Prompt:** what you type.\n- **System prompt or custom instructions:** standing text the product places in front of every prompt to shape behaviour. The laminated card.\n- **Context window:** the model's desk. All the text it can consider at once, measured in tokens.\n- **Iteration:** correcting and re-running. The real skill of prompting.\n- **Grounding instruction:** telling the model to answer only from material you provided, and to say so when the material runs out. The open-book exam. Reduces invention, doesn't eliminate it.\n- **Project, Gem, notebook, GPT:** product features (the names vary by vendor) that bundle standing instructions and files into a reusable workspace. The office library.\n- **Retrieval:** when a product with many stored files fetches just the relevant pages for each question instead of loading everything. The librarian.\n- **Canvas or Artifacts:** an editable document pane beside the chat, so you revise output in place.\n- **Live search:** a feature that fetches current web pages for questions about recent events. Check anything that matters."}, {"slug": "class-5-software-without-the-software-bill", "sourceFile": "05-class-5-software-without-the-software-bill.md", "title": "Class 5: Software Without the Software Bill", "subtitle": "The collapsed cost of custom software, AI-assisted building, and what is realistic for a ten-person business", "md": "\n**Time:** about 20 minutes of reading, plus a 30-minute build-or-decide exercise.\n**By the end of this class you can:** explain why custom software used to cost six figures and now can cost an afternoon, sort any build idea into one of three tiers with honest boundaries, and run a four-week path from annoying process to working internal tool.\n\n## The short version\n\nSoftware tailored to your business used to cost a fortune because skilled people had to write every line. AI now writes much of the routine code, so a small internal tool can take an afternoon. But a tool that works once on your screen is not the same as one your business can rely on, so build freely for low-stakes internal jobs and bring in a professional for anything touching money or customers' personal information.\n\n## Why software was expensive\n\nThink back to the photocopier. The expensive part of a document was never the copy, it was writing the original. Once written, each extra copy cost a sheet of paper.\n\nSoftware in 1995 had expensive originals and expensive copies. Writing it meant developers billing by the month. Running it meant buying and housing your own servers. Each user needed a licence, and each copy shipped on disks with a printed manual.\n\nBecause every copy and every user cost real money, the industry built software once and sold it to thousands. Nobody customised it for a ten-person business. So small businesses bent their processes around whatever came off the shelf, because bending the software was unaffordable. Remember that shape: the last thirty years have taken it apart piece by piece.\n\n## What collapsed, and in what order\n\nPicture opening a restaurant.\n\n1. **Renting the kitchen got cheap.** Instead of building your own commercial kitchen, you hire one by the hour. In software, that's cloud computing: servers became an hourly rental, so running a tool for ten people costs next to nothing.\n2. **Delivery became free.** The internet made copying software free. The cost of one extra copy or one extra user, which economists call the **marginal cost**, is now close to zero.\n3. **Now the cooking got cheap.** AI coding assistants are the sous-chef doing the prep. Tools such as GitHub Copilot, Cursor, Claude Code, and the code modes inside ChatGPT, Gemini and Copilot write, explain and fix routine code from plain-English requests.\n\nCopying and running software was already nearly free. Now the expensive human work of writing it has become dramatically cheaper as well. Put together, that means customised software costs a fraction of what it did, and that is the biggest shift happening right now. It's bigger than any robot story, and it's the whole of this class.\n\n## What that means for a ten-person business\n\nThese are real examples, not fantasies:\n\n- A quoting tool that turns your standard rates and job types into branded PDF quotes.\n- A job-intake form that fills a tidy spreadsheet and drafts the confirmation email.\n- A search across ten years of PDFs, policies and supplier catalogues. This is also the best use for a local model from Class 3.\n- A booking page for your niche that no software company bothered to build.\n- A small program that renames, sorts and backs up the photos from every job.\n\nIn 2010, each of these was a $50,000 to $200,000 conversation. Today each is an afternoon to a weekend with an AI assistant. For anything customers use, add a few hundred dollars of professional review and hosting.\n\n## A worked example, start to finish\n\nHere's what a small build actually feels like.\n\nYou open a chat assistant and describe the tool in plain English: \"Build me a one-page web tool where I enter a customer name, an invoice amount and days overdue, and it writes my reminder email in my usual tone. Internal use, fake data only.\" The assistant writes the code, roughly a hundred lines in a single file. You save it, open it in your web browser, and it works. Ten minutes, start to finish, for the demo version.\n\nNow the honest part, which is the real lesson. That demo has no database, no logins, no backups and no security check. It lives wherever you saved it. If it breaks, nobody is on call.\n\nTurning it into software the business relies on means adding **hosting** (a rented computer where the tool lives, so other people can reach it), logins if anyone outside you will use it, backups, and a person who owns the problem when it fails at 4pm on a Friday. The gap between \"works in the demo\" and \"safe to rely on\" is where judgement lives. The three tiers below keep that judgement honest.\n\n## The three tiers: the DIY rules for software\n\nYou already know the Australian rules for DIY around the house. You can change a light bulb. You can build a garden shed. You can't do your own wiring: that needs a licensed electrician, however good your YouTube research was. Software works the same way.\n\n**Tier one, the light bulb: no build.** Set up tools you already pay for, such as a Copilot agent over your SharePoint, a Gemini Gem over your Drive, or an automation with an AI step. Most \"we should build something\" ideas end here, cheaply.\n\n**Tier two, the garden shed: assisted build.** Describe a small tool to an AI assistant, keep it internal and low-stakes, use fake data, and accept that you can't check the code yourself. This is where your napkin ideas live, and it's genuinely safe as long as the stakes stay low.\n\n**Tier three, the wiring: real software.** Anything that takes payments, holds lots of customer data, or that the business would really hurt to lose. Tier three always needs a professional involved, even if AI wrote 90 percent of the code, because someone has to own security, backups and support. A few hundred dollars of review isn't an insult to the AI's work. It's insurance on yours.\n\n## \"Vibe coding\", named and bounded\n\n**Vibe coding** is the popular name for describing software in plain language and accepting whatever code comes back, without reading it.\n\nIt's like assembling flat-pack furniture without looking at the instructions. For a bookshelf in the shed, who cares if a screw is in the wrong hole. For a child's cot, you read every step and check every bolt.\n\nVibe coding works astonishingly well for **prototypes** (first working versions, built to learn from, not to rely on) and for internal tools. It produces software the builder can't check, which is fine at tier two and dangerous at tier three.\n\nThe rule this class teaches: vibe-code freely for internal, low-stakes tools. Never vibe-code anything that holds other people's personal information, takes payments, or would hurt to lose. An AI-written app that works in the demo isn't the same as one that's secure, backed up and maintainable, and the difference only shows up under stress.\n\n## Cloud versus local for building software\n\nThe same Class 3 rules apply to code as to any other data.\n\n**Cloud coding tools** have the most capable engines, and your code, sometimes your whole project, goes to the vendor. So check the account tier's training and retention settings exactly as you would for customer data. Business tiers generally exclude your code from training. Consumer tiers may not.\n\n**Local coding tools** mean running a coding model on your own computer, through Ollama or LM Studio, inside your code editor. Your code never leaves the building. That matters for agencies under non-disclosure agreements, government-related work, and firms with strict client confidentiality rules. The trade-off is capability: local coding models are fine for routine code and explanations, and noticeably weaker on big rewrites and hard bugs.\n\nThe mix most small firms land on: local or business-tier tools for client code, and consumer cloud tools for learning and throwaway experiments.\n\n## The cost reality check\n\nWhat it actually costs in 2026:\n\n- An AI subscription: roughly $20 to $30 per person per month, or free tiers for light use.\n- Hosting for a small internal tool: free to about $30 per month.\n- A domain name: $15 to $25 per year.\n- Your time, or a junior's.\n- For tier three, a professional review: a few hundred to a few thousand dollars. Still a rounding error against the 1995 numbers.\n\nBudget honestly for the line everyone forgets: **maintenance**. Software is a plant, not a purchase. It needs watering. Your tool leans on other people's free building blocks, called **dependencies**, and like car parts those occasionally get recalled and need replacing. Nobody updates what nobody owns.\n\n## Buying versus building, the updated rule\n\nThe old rule was \"never build what you can buy.\" The new rule:\n\n- **Buy** anything standard, such as email, accounting and payroll.\n- **Build** the 10 percent that's genuinely specific to how your business works.\n- **Never build** anything regulated yourself, such as payments or health records.\n\nAI-assisted building moved the line. It didn't erase it.\n\n## The traps, named so you can step over them\n\n- **Security left off by default.** AI-built apps often ship without logins, without checking what people type in, and with **secrets** handled carelessly. Secrets are the passwords and access keys software needs and attackers want; leaving them in the code is a key under the doormat. Ask for security explicitly, and have a professional check.\n- **The laptop problem.** \"It only runs on Brad's laptop\" is an outage waiting to happen. Host it properly, or accept it's a prototype forever.\n- **Data leaking during the build.** Pasting your real customer database into a consumer chatbot so it can help build the tool is the Class 3 mistake in a new costume. Fake data in while building; real data later, in your own systems.\n- **The maintenance cliff.** The person who built it leaves, and the business inherits software nobody can explain. Write a half-page note while you remember: what it does, where it lives, what usually breaks first.\n\n## A realistic path, four weeks\n\n- **Week one:** pick one annoying internal process, the one everyone grumbles about.\n- **Week two:** prototype it at tier two, with fake data.\n- **Week three:** use it daily and note every failure. The failure list becomes your specification.\n- **Week four:** decide. Keep it as it is, park it, or budget a professional to make it production-ready: secure, backed up, monitored and supported.\n\nTotal cost before you decide: a subscription and a few evenings. You can repeat that path for every idea on your napkin.\n\n## What this does to the software you already pay for\n\nOne level-headed note about the market. When custom software is cheap, generic software that just turns a paper form into a screen comes under pressure. Vendors respond by bolting on AI features, which is the \"powered by AI\" translation exercise from Class 2.\n\nYou now have a strong question at every renewal: what would this cost to build for our specific way of working? Sometimes the answer is still \"buy\". At least now it's a real comparison instead of a shrug.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"Only programmers can build software, so non-technical owners should stay out of it.\"** That was true when building meant years of specialised work. It stopped being true in stages, and AI coding tools removed most of what remained.\n\nA non-technical owner who can describe their process precisely, who knows exactly what happens between a customer's phone call and the job sheet, can now produce working internal tools with a chat assistant, without reading a line of the code.\n\nThe myth survives because it suits software vendors, and because the real caveats are easy to confuse with \"you can't\". The honest statement is narrower and more useful: you can build internal, low-stakes tools yourself this month. You shouldn't build anything that holds other people's personal information, takes payments, or that the business would suffer to lose, without a professional owning security and support. Inside that boundary, the person closest to the problem is often the best person to build the fix.\n\n## Your exercise: napkin to prototype\n\n1. Write the most annoying repetitive process in your work on one card, in three sentences, as if explaining it to a new employee. If your first draft is vague, rewrite it.\n2. Turn the card into a build request using this template: what the tool takes in, what it does, what it produces, who uses it, plus the line \"this is an internal prototype, use fake data only\".\n3. Run it in any assistant that can write code. Save the result as a single HTML file if offered, and open it in your browser. If something breaks, paste the error message back in and ask for a fix. That loop is the whole skill.\n4. Use the tool once on a realistic but fake example, and write down the first thing you'd fix.\n5. Make the tier call in one sentence: usable internally next week as it is, or needs a professional before anyone outside the business touches it?\n\nThe gap between those two lists is the lesson of this class.\n\n## Explain it back\n\nYour business partner asks: \"If AI can write software now, why would we ever pay a developer again?\" In three sentences, with no jargon, give them the honest answer. Then compare it with \"The short version\" and the DIY rules.\n\nIf you couldn't explain why it got cheaper, reread the restaurant. If you weren't sure where the line sits, reread the shed and the wiring.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can explain why custom software got cheap, in one sentence, to a colleague.\n- I have one napkin idea sorted into a tier, with reasons.\n- I know which traps my idea would walk into, and the first step over each.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Marginal cost:** what one extra copy or one extra user costs. For software in 2026, near zero. The whole story of this class.\n- **AI coding assistant:** a tool that writes, explains and fixes code from plain-English requests. The sous-chef.\n- **Vibe coding:** describing software conversationally and accepting the code without reading it. Flat-pack without the instructions. Fine for prototypes and internal tools, never for sensitive or customer-facing work.\n- **Prototype:** a first working version, built to learn from, not to rely on.\n- **Hosting:** the rented computer where a tool lives so other people can reach it.\n- **Dependencies:** other people's free code building blocks that your tool relies on. They need occasional updates, like recalled car parts.\n- **Secrets:** passwords, access keys and similar credentials. Store them properly, never paste them into chat tools.\n- **Production-ready:** a prototype that has been made secure, backed up, monitored and supported, usually by a professional. Also called \"productionising\" it.\n- **Local LLM-assisted development:** running a coding model on your own hardware inside your code editor, so your code never leaves the building."}, {"slug": "class-6-keeping-your-house-in-order", "sourceFile": "06-class-6-keeping-your-house-in-order.md", "title": "Class 6: Keeping Your House in Order", "subtitle": "Shadow AI, data security basics, and the paperwork that protects a small business", "md": "\n**Time:** about 20 minutes of reading, plus a 30-minute rollout draft.\n**By the end of this class you can:** explain shadow AI without villainising anyone, run a one-week governance rollout for a business up to about fifty people, answer \"did you use AI on my file?\" honestly and calmly, and name the single role that makes all of it stick.\n\n## The short version\n\nYour staff are already using AI, whether or not you've said they can. Banning it just hides it. Instead, approve a few safe tools, write one page of rules, make it easy and blame-free to own up to mistakes, and put one named person in charge. That takes a week.\n\n## Start with the uncomfortable truth\n\nYour staff are already using AI. Every workplace survey since 2023 has found the same thing: use runs well ahead of policy, everywhere, including in businesses that think they've banned it.\n\nThe only open question is whether you know where it's happening, with what data, and what happens when something goes wrong. This class closes that gap in one week, without blame, because blame is what keeps the next mistake hidden.\n\n## Shadow AI, defined plainly\n\nPicture a staff member who uses their own ute for a work delivery because the van was booked. They're being helpful. But the business doesn't know, the insurance doesn't cover it, and if something goes wrong, nobody finds out until it's a problem.\n\n**Shadow AI** is the same thing with AI tools: staff using them for work without the business knowing or approving. Pasting the client list into a free chatbot to tidy it up. Drafting a tender response on a personal account at the kitchen table. Summarising a roster that includes everyone's home addresses in a phone app.\n\nIt's called \"shadow\" because the business can't see it, not because the staff are villains. Usually they're being enterprising with the tools they have, which is a trait you otherwise want.\n\n## Why it's a genuine risk, in three lines\n\n- **Data risk.** Free consumer tools may keep what you paste, may use it to improve their models unless a switch says otherwise, sit on overseas computers, and come with no contract you can point to.\n- **Accuracy risk.** Unchecked output reaches customers. The Deloitte and Air Canada cases in Class 2 showed what that looks like at scale.\n- **Legal risk.** Under the Privacy Act, you stay responsible for the personal information your business collects, including when an employee sends it to a US chatbot from their phone. That's the APP 8 and APP 11 point from Class 3.\n\n## Why bans fail, and what works instead\n\nBanning AI in 2026 is like banning the photocopier in 1986. You can't enforce it, you can't see whether it's working, and it guarantees that whatever use does happen is hidden, which is exactly the risk you were trying to reduce.\n\nWhat works is an **amnesty plus approved tools**. Think of a library fine amnesty: bring the overdue books back, no fine, and the library finally learns what's been missing. Here's the business version:\n\n1. Approve two or three tools, on business tiers.\n2. Publish a one-page policy saying what may and may not be pasted where.\n3. Set up a no-blame way to report \"oops\" moments.\n4. Ask teams what they're already using, with no consequences for honesty. Turn the useful habits into official ones, and move the risky ones to the right tool.\n\nDocument 3 is exactly this policy, written to be handed out rather than filed.\n\n## The four habits that prevent almost every incident\n\nEvery commercial kitchen runs on a few food-safety habits: wash your hands, separate raw and cooked, check the temperature, and report a cut straight away. Nobody finds them exciting, and they prevent almost every disaster. AI at work has its own four:\n\n1. **Work data on work accounts.** Only use business-tier accounts or local tools for work data, because that's where the contract protections are. Check which account you're signed into before you paste, every time. The icons look alike.\n2. **The decision drill before pasting.** Personal information, secrets, or anything under a non-disclosure agreement? If yes, change tool or remove the sensitive parts. It's from Class 3, repeated here because it does most of the work.\n3. **A person checks everything that leaves the building.** Names, numbers, dates, quotes, references. Checking is usually faster than doing the task from scratch, which is why the honest claim is \"faster with a review\", not \"automatic\".\n4. **Report the same day.** If something sensitive went where it shouldn't, tell the policy owner that day, with no drama. It's like reporting a scratch on the work car: reported on the day it's a note in a logbook, discovered a month later it's a problem. Delay is the only thing that turns an incident into a crisis.\n\n## The one-page policy, walked through\n\nEvery workplace has a \"how we do things here\" sheet on the staffroom wall. Document 3 is that sheet for AI. Read it once, properly.\n\nIt covers where the business stands (\"we want you using these tools well, not secretly\"), the approved tools, what may be pasted, what must never be pasted, the checking rule, the same-day \"oops\" procedure, and being honest with clients.\n\nYour job this week is to make it yours. Replace the bracketed business name and contact person, cross out anything that doesn't apply, and add one rule specific to your industry. A medical centre adds \"no patient identifiers, ever\". A law firm adds \"nothing covered by client privilege without the principal's approval\".\n\nOne page, one read, one signature line. Anything longer doesn't get read, which is why it's one page.\n\n## The incident card, walked through\n\nThink of the evacuation map by the lift. You read it once, calmly, so that on the bad day you don't have to think.\n\nDocument 10 is that map for AI incidents. It covers the four that actually happen, each as a short, calm checklist:\n\n- An employee realises they pasted sensitive data into a consumer tool.\n- A client asks whether AI was used on their work.\n- A vendor breach hits the news.\n- Wrong AI output went to a customer.\n\nRead all four once before you need them, then pin the card where people can see it. Rehearse one out loud. Really, out loud. The scenario most worth practising is the client question, and the honest answer, prepared in advance, is: \"Yes, where it was used, here's what a person checked, and here's our policy.\" Delivered calmly, that answer builds trust. Improvised under pressure, the same facts sound like a confession.\n\n## Keeping track of your tools\n\nYou won't run big-company procurement, and you don't need to. Three habits carry most of the value:\n\n- **Keep a tool register.** One line per AI tool in use: name, tier, who uses it, and what data touches it. It's an asset register, like the list of who has which laptop.\n- **Check before you buy.** Run the Class 2 vendor questions and get the business-tier terms and data processing agreement in writing.\n- **Review it every quarter,** in the same calendar slot as the pricing and privacy-settings review from Documents 5 and 6, because tiers, prices and policies all change.\n\n## Disclosure: when to say AI was involved\n\nThere's no blanket Australian law requiring ordinary business documents to be labelled as AI-assisted. Specific duties are tightening anyway, so work from principles:\n\n- **Disclose when asked,** honestly and without theatre.\n- **Disclose up front where the work is advice:** legal, accounting, medical, engineering, or anything safety-related.\n- **Follow any contract that restricts AI use.** Breaching one is a commercial and legal problem regardless of AI law, as Deloitte discovered.\n- **Never imply a person checked work that nobody checked.** Misleading-conduct rules apply. More importantly, being caught ends relationships that honest disclosure would have strengthened.\n\n## One person in charge\n\nEvery workplace has a named fire warden. Not because they'll fight the fire alone, but because on the day, everyone knows who's checking the floor.\n\nName one person to own the policy, the tool register, the quarterly review and the incident log. In a ten-person business, that's often the owner or the office manager. Give them the document pack and half an hour a month.\n\nOwnership is the difference between paperwork and practice. Every failed rollout in every industry comes down to nobody owning it, and every successful one comes down to somebody doing exactly this.\n\n## The regulatory horizon, briefly\n\nAustralian privacy reform is arriving in stages. The direction is clear even where the dates aren't.\n\n- The first round of Privacy Act changes is in force, with stronger powers for the regulator, higher penalties, and since June 2025, the right to sue for serious invasions of privacy.\n- The duty to explain automated decision-making in privacy policies arrives on 10 December 2026.\n- Further rounds, including the future of the small-business exemption, are still in the policy pipeline.\n\nThe sensible stance is to run your business as if the exemption won't save you, because it's shrinking and because good practice here is cheap: one page, one owner, one week.\n\n## The one-week rollout plan\n\n- **Day one:** pick your named owner and your two or three approved tools.\n- **Day two:** circulate Document 3 with a covering note that says, in your own words, \"we want you using these well, not secretly\".\n- **Day three:** collect the amnesty answers. What's already in use, no consequences. Turn the useful parts into official practice.\n- **Day four:** move approved tools onto business tiers, or set up the local option. Apply the opt-out settings from Document 5 to anything still on consumer accounts in the meantime.\n- **Day five:** pin Document 10 where people can see it, and run a ten-minute **toolbox talk** (a short team briefing, usually at the start of a shift) using Document 8, the plain-English FAQ.\n\nThat's a complete AI governance rollout for a business of up to about fifty people, done in a week, for the cost of a subscription or two. No version of this needs a consultant, a project board or a quarter.\n\n## Myth, meet reality\n\n**\"We're too small to be a target, so AI data risks don't apply to us.\"** A burglar walking down the street tries every door handle. They don't check how many staff work inside first.\n\nAttackers and data brokers pick victims by weakness, not size. A small business with staff pasting client data into free chatbots, no written policy, no tool register and no incident plan is weak in exactly the ways that matter. Human error and stolen passwords cause most real incidents, far more than movie-style hacking, and a chatbot account on a personal email with a reused password sits squarely in that category.\n\n\"Too small\" also fails as a legal defence. The Privacy Act's core duties already apply to many small organisations regardless of turnover, the right to sue for serious invasions of privacy has no turnover test, and your clients increasingly pass their obligations down to you through contracts. In a tender, \"we're too small for that\" loses to a competitor holding this course's one-page policy.\n\nThe right conclusion isn't fear. It's that the controls a small business needs are proportionately small: one page, one owner, one week, and this class hands you all three.\n\n## Your exercise: draft your rollout, tonight\n\n1. Fill in the rollout worksheet in this course: named owner, approved tools and their tiers, the first line of your tool register, who to tell about \"oops\" moments, and the date of next week's toolbox talk.\n2. Mark up Document 3 with your details: business name, contact person, your one industry-specific rule, and cross out anything that doesn't apply.\n3. Choose one scenario from Document 10 and read its checklist out loud to someone. Let them ask one \"but what if\" question, and answer it without looking. Swap and repeat with a second scenario.\n4. Write the first sentence you'll say to your team on Monday. Say it out loud once. If it sounds like a police announcement, rewrite it until it sounds like an invitation, because the amnesty only works if people believe it.\n\n## Explain it back\n\nAt Monday's team meeting, someone asks: \"Are we in trouble for using ChatGPT?\" In three sentences, with no jargon, answer them in a way that's honest, calm, and makes them want to tell you more. Then compare it with \"The short version\" and the amnesty.\n\nIf your answer sounded like a warning, reread \"Why bans fail\". If you couldn't name the four habits, reread the kitchen.\n\n## Before you move on\n\n- I can define shadow AI without blaming anyone.\n- My named owner, approved tools and reporting line are written down.\n- I have rehearsed the client-question answer out loud at least once.\n\n## Words used in this class\n\n- **Shadow AI:** AI tools used for work without the business knowing or approving. Invisible use, not malicious use. The personal ute on a work delivery.\n- **AI use policy:** the one page telling staff which tools are approved, what may be pasted, what must be checked, and who to tell. Document 3.\n- **Tool register:** one line per tool: name, tier, who uses it, what data touches it, when it was last reviewed.\n- **Amnesty:** a no-consequences window for staff to declare the AI tools they already use, so the use can be made official or corrected. The single most effective fix for shadow AI.\n- **Incident:** sensitive data pasted where it shouldn't have been, wrong output reaching a customer, or a vendor breach affecting you. Document 10 turns each into a checklist.\n- **Owner:** the one named person accountable for the policy, register, reviews and incident log. The fire warden. Without this role, everything else is decoration.\n- **Disclosure:** telling a client, customer or reader that AI was involved in producing work. Required by some contracts, and a trust-builder almost everywhere else.\n- **Flow-down obligations:** requirements your clients or funders write into contracts that become your requirements. They increasingly include AI rules.\n- **OAIC:** the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner, the privacy regulator. Its generative AI guidance is the plainest official statement of what \"reasonable steps\" look like.\n- **Toolbox talk:** a short team briefing, five to fifteen minutes, usually at the start of a shift. The right format for rolling out Documents 3, 8 and 10."}], "docs": [{"slug": "doc-01-system-prompt-starter-kit", "sourceFile": "07-doc-01-system-prompt-starter-kit.md", "title": "Document 1: System Prompt Starter Kit", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini and Gems (Google), Copilot (Microsoft), and local tools such as Ollama and LM Studio running Meta's Llama or similar open models.\n**Version:** October 2026. Interfaces change; the concepts do not.\n\n---\n\n## In plain terms\n\nA system prompt is a short standing briefing that your AI tool reads before every request, so you stop repeating yourself. Write it once, paste it into the right setting, and every draft starts closer to how your business actually talks.\n\n## What a system prompt is\n\nPicture the laminated card you'd leave on a new temp's desk: who we are, who our customers are, how we write, and the things we never do. They read it every morning without being asked.\n\nA system prompt is that card for your AI tool. You still type your request as usual. The system prompt is written once and quietly placed in front of every request, and the tool treats it as more important than any single message. That makes it the right place for the rules you're tired of repeating: who your business is, how you want things written, and what the tool must never do.\n\nThe difference from a real temp: this one never forgets the card, and never takes a sickie.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nWithout one, every chat starts from zero. The tool guesses your tone, your audience and your habits, and you spend your first three messages correcting it. With a good system prompt, the first draft lands much closer to usable. It's the cheapest, fastest quality upgrade in any of these products, and almost nobody uses it properly.\n\n**What it can't do:** a system prompt can't make the tool know more, and it can't stop it inventing facts. It shapes behaviour, tone, format and priorities. You still check the output.\n\n**Why it should be short:** the card sits on the model's desk in every conversation, taking up space. Keep it to a briefing, not a novel. About 250 words is plenty.\n\n## What it's called on each platform\n\nEvery product has this feature under a different name.\n\n| Platform | What the feature is called | Where it lives (as at October 2026) |\n|---|---|---|\n| ChatGPT | Custom instructions, plus per-Project instructions | Settings, then Personalisation, then Custom instructions. Two boxes: \"What would you like ChatGPT to know about you?\" and \"How would you like ChatGPT to respond?\". Separately: any Project you create has its own instructions box that applies only inside that Project. |\n| Claude | Profile / personal instructions, plus per-Project custom instructions | Settings, then Profile, \"What personal preferences should Claude consider in responses?\" Projects: open the Project, then the instructions field in Project settings. |\n| Gemini | Personal context, plus Gems (custom assistants) | Settings, then Personal context (a single \"what should Gemini know\" box). Gems: create a Gem and paste your system prompt into its instructions; that Gem then behaves that way every time you open it. |\n| Copilot (consumer) | Personalisation / preferred responses | In the Copilot app or copilot.microsoft.com: Settings, then Personalisation. |\n| Microsoft 365 Copilot (work account) | Usually set by your IT admin; users get limited personal preferences | Admins can configure organisational prompts and agents in the Microsoft 365 admin centre and Copilot Studio. Individual staff typically cannot override them, which is the point. |\n| Workspace (Gemini in Gmail/Docs) | No user system prompt inside Gmail/Docs drafting; use Gems for standing behaviour | Create a Gem with your template, or paste your instructions at the top of a \"Help me write\" prompt. |\n| Ollama / LM Studio (local models) | System prompt field in the chat settings | LM Studio: the chat screen has a system prompt box on the right-hand settings panel. Ollama: pass a system prompt when you run the model, or use any front-end app that exposes the field. |\n\n**Differences worth knowing:**\n\n- **ChatGPT's custom instructions apply to every chat.** Handy, but annoying when you want work and personal kept apart. Use a Project for work.\n- **Claude's Projects** are the strongest way to keep business contexts separate: one Project per type of client, each with its own instructions and files. Think of them as separate filing drawers.\n- **Gems** are the same idea in Google's world, and live inside your Google account.\n- **Microsoft 365 Copilot** deliberately gives standing instructions to your organisation's admin rather than to each person. That's good governance (one version of the truth for the whole team), and occasionally frustrating.\n\n---\n\n## Template 1: Small retail business\n\nPaste into your platform's system prompt / custom instructions / Gem instructions field. Edit the square brackets.\n\n```\nYou assist [Business Name], an independent [retail type, e.g. hardware store / boutique / gift shop] in [town/suburb], with a team of [number]. We sell [main product categories] to [customer types, e.g. local tradies, gift buyers, tourists].\n\nWhen writing for us:\n- Plain Australian English. Warm but not gushy. Short sentences.\n- Customers come first: product names, prices and promotions must match exactly what I give you. Never invent prices, stock levels, dates or product claims.\n- If I ask for social media copy: [platform, e.g. Facebook and Instagram], maximum [number] sentences, one clear call to action, no hashtags unless I ask.\n- If I ask for customer emails: friendly, brief, answer the customer's actual problem, sign off as [name/role].\n- For supplier or landlord correspondence: polite, factual, firm, no slang.\n- Never promise refunds, exchanges, warranties or delivery dates unless I have given you the policy in the message. If the answer depends on our policy and I have not supplied it, ask me.\n- When you are unsure, say so plainly instead of guessing.\n- Do not use the words \"delve\", \"elevate\", \"game-changing\" or \"unlock\".\n```\n\n## Template 2: Professional services firm (accounting, legal, consulting)\n\n```\nYou assist [Firm Name], a [discipline] firm in [city] with [number] staff. Our clients are [client types, e.g. small businesses, individuals, SMEs in the construction sector]. Our tone is [e.g. precise and reassuring].\n\nRules:\n- Plain Australian English, professional register. Define any technical term the first time you use it.\n- You are a drafting assistant, not an adviser. You do not provide accounting, legal or financial advice, and you must never state a rule of law, a tax rate, a threshold or a deadline as fact unless I supplied it in the conversation. Where a claim needs verification, mark it [VERIFY] in the output.\n- Structure documents clearly: headings, short paragraphs, numbered lists for steps.\n- When summarising material I provide, distinguish clearly between what the document says and what you infer.\n- Flag, in a separate line at the end, anything in my material that looks time-sensitive, jurisdiction-specific, or beyond your knowledge, so a qualified human reviews it before it goes to a client.\n- Confidentiality: I will only paste client material that our firm's AI policy permits. Never assume context I have not given.\n- Output defaults: emails under 150 words; memos with a three-sentence executive summary up top; tables where data is involved.\n```\n\n## Template 3: Trades or field-services business\n\n```\nYou assist [Business Name], a [trade, e.g. plumbing / electrical / landscaping] business based in [region], with [number] on the tools and [number] in the office. Our customers are [e.g. homeowners, builders, property managers] across [service area].\n\nWhen writing for us:\n- Straight-talking Australian English. No corporate fluff, no jargon the customer would not use. Read it out loud; if a tradie would not say it, rewrite it.\n- Quotes and job descriptions: use only the figures, dates and scope I give you. Never estimate prices, materials or timeframes yourself. If something is missing, list what you need before drafting.\n- Customer emails and texts: short, friendly, confirm the facts (what, when, how much), and always include what happens next.\n- Job sheet and work-completion summaries: bullet points, plain language a homeowner understands, note anything the customer should watch for.\n- Supplier and subcontractor messages: direct, factual, no apologies we do not owe.\n- Compliance language (certificates, standards, permits): never paraphrase a requirement from memory. Use only what I paste in, and mark anything else [CHECK WITH LICENSED PERSON].\n- If I dictate rough notes, turn them into clean text without adding facts.\n```\n\n---\n\n## How to install your template, step by step\n\n**ChatGPT.** Open Settings (your profile picture or name: bottom left on the web, top right on mobile). Choose Personalisation, then Custom instructions. Paste the template into the second box (\"How would you like ChatGPT to respond?\"), and put one or two lines about your business into the first box. Save.\n\nFor a dedicated work space, create a Project (sidebar, plus button), open its settings, and paste the full template into Project instructions. Files you add to that Project stay available to every chat inside it. On consumer plans, also check Settings, Data controls, and turn off model training if you haven't already.\n\n**Claude.** Open Settings, then Profile. Paste your template into the personal preferences field. To keep contexts separate, create a Project (sidebar), open Project settings, and paste the template into the Project's custom instructions. Add reference files to the Project knowledge as needed.\n\n**Gemini.** Two options.\n- *Quick:* Settings, Personal context. Paste a shortened version, because this field is smaller than a full template. Keep the identity line and the top five rules.\n- *Better for business:* go to Gems (sidebar, or gemini.google.com), create a Gem, name it something like \"[Business] assistant\", paste the full template as its instructions, and use that Gem for work tasks. Workspace users can also use the same Gems inside Google apps, depending on plan.\n\n**Copilot (consumer).** In the Copilot app or website, open Settings, then Personalisation, and paste what the field accepts.\n\n**Microsoft 365 Copilot (work).** Individual staff usually can't set an organisation-wide system prompt. Send your template to whoever looks after Microsoft 365 and ask for it to be set up centrally (in Copilot Studio or the admin prompt settings). That's a feature, not a bug: one version of the truth for the whole team.\n\n**Local (Ollama / LM Studio).** In LM Studio's chat screen, open the settings panel on the right and paste the template into the System prompt field. It applies to that chat and can be saved with the preset. In Ollama itself, the system prompt is set per session or in a settings file called a Modelfile; most people use a front-end app that gives them the box. Bonus: your instructions, like everything else, stay on your machine.\n\n## Writing your own: the five-part formula\n\nEvery template above has the same skeleton. Yours should too:\n\n1. **Identity.** Who we are, in one sentence.\n2. **Audience.** Who we write to.\n3. **Tone.** Three or four concrete rules, not adjectives. \"Short sentences, no exclamation marks\" works. \"Professional\" doesn't, because the tool has to guess what you mean by it.\n4. **Formats.** Your default length and layout for each type of task.\n5. **Never rules.** The bright lines: no invented prices, no advice beyond your licence, no compliance wording from memory, and \"say you're not sure\" instead of guessing.\n\nKeep it under about 250 words. Remember that it sits on the desk in every conversation.\n\n## Testing it\n\nAfter installing, run three everyday tasks: draft a customer email, summarise a document, write a social post. Compare them with what you used to get.\n\nThen try to break it on purpose. Ask for something your never-rules forbid, such as \"quote a price for this job\", and check that it asks you for the figures instead of inventing them. If it invents them, strengthen that line in the template. This ten-minute test is worth more than any prompt-engineering course."}, {"slug": "doc-02-data-ingestion-guide", "sourceFile": "08-doc-02-data-ingestion-guide.md", "title": "Document 2: How to Show Your AI Stuff (Data Ingestion Guide)", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini and NotebookLM (Google), Copilot and Microsoft 365 Copilot (Microsoft), and local tools (Ollama, LM Studio) with any chat front-end that accepts files.\n**Version:** October 2026.\n\n---\n\n## In plain terms\n\nAn AI tool only knows what you put in front of it right now. There are four ways to put things in front of it, from handing over one page to giving it a key to your filing room. Pick the smallest one that does the job, and screen for anything sensitive first.\n\n## The one idea behind all of this\n\nA model only knows what's on its desk right now. \"Showing your AI stuff\" just means putting the right material on that desk: your price list, the client's email, last year's report, the policy document.\n\nEvery feature in this guide (pasting, uploading, projects, connectors, knowledge bases) is a different way of getting material onto the desk. They differ in how long the material stays there and how much the tool can reach.\n\n**Before you show it anything,** run the decision drill from Class 3: does this contain personal information, secrets, or anything under a non-disclosure agreement? If yes, use a business-tier tool, a contracted Microsoft or Google work account, or a local tool. Free consumer accounts are for material you wouldn't mind a stranger reading, because you can't fully un-send it.\n\n## The four ways to give a tool context\n\nThink of how you'd give information to a temp.\n\n**1. Pasting into the chat: handing over one sheet of paper.** The oldest and most reliable method. Copy the text, paste it, done. It's on the desk straight away, all of it, exactly as you gave it.\n- *Downsides:* it's gone when the chat ends. Very long pastes take up desk space and can push earlier parts of the conversation off the edge. Formatting, especially tables, can get scrambled.\n- *Best for:* one-off material under a few pages.\n\n**2. Uploading a file to the chat: handing over a folder.** The paperclip or plus button. The product turns your file into text (and for images, describes or reads them) and puts it on the desk. Good for PDFs, Word files, spreadsheets and photos of documents.\n- *What happens underneath:* the text is pulled out of the file. That's near-perfect for clean digital documents and patchy for tables and diagrams. For scans and photos, the tool has to read a picture of text, which is called **OCR** (optical character recognition). Like reading a faded fax, it can misread.\n- *Big files get skimmed:* very large files may be read in slices rather than all at once, so \"read this 300-page PDF and tell me everything\" can quietly become \"read part of it\". Ask pointed questions about specific sections and you'll get much better results.\n\n**3. Adding to a Project, Gem, Notebook or knowledge base: the office library.** The long-term option. ChatGPT Projects, Claude Projects, Google Gems and NotebookLM, and Microsoft 365 Copilot over SharePoint all let you add files and instructions once, and every conversation in that space can use them.\n- *What happens underneath:* most products don't keep the whole library on the desk at once. For each question, a librarian fetches the relevant pages and puts just those on the desk. That's called **retrieval**.\n- *What that means for you:* excellent for a standing body of material, such as your policies, product catalogue and templates. It's slightly less exact than a direct paste, so for the one document that matters most right now, also paste or attach it to the chat and say \"use the attached version as the authority\".\n\n**4. Connecting live data sources: giving it a key to your filing room.** The products increasingly plug straight into your systems:\n- Microsoft 365 Copilot reads your Outlook, Teams, Word, Excel and SharePoint content, respecting the permissions already set. That's its big advantage.\n- Gemini can work across Gmail, Drive, Docs and Calendar for Workspace accounts.\n- ChatGPT and Claude offer connectors for Drive, Gmail, SharePoint and common business apps on some tiers.\n\nIt's powerful and convenient, and it changes the question from \"what did I paste?\" to \"what can the tool reach?\". The key opens every door your own key opens, including that spreadsheet you forgot you had access to. Turn on only the connections you actually use, and prefer work accounts with admin controls.\n\n## Side by side\n\n| | Pasted into a chat | Uploaded to this chat | In a Project / Gem / Notebook | Live connector |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lifespan | This conversation only | This conversation only | The workspace, across conversations | Ongoing, live |\n| Fidelity | Exactly what you gave | Text-extracted; tables and scans can degrade | Extracted plus retrieval; may quote indirectly | Depends on the app |\n| Best for | The one crucial document, right now | PDFs, photos, spreadsheets for a task | Standing reference: policies, catalogues, templates | Working where your data already lives |\n| Risk to manage | Chat history retention | Same, plus file stored in vendor systems | Everything in the space is reachable by everyone in the space (shared projects especially) | Scope creep: it can reach more than you think |\n\nThe common beginner mistake is connecting a whole library when one precise paste would do. The common advanced mistake is pasting the same 20-page document into every new chat instead of putting it in a Project once. Rule of thumb: the library for standing material, a pasted page for today's facts.\n\n## What the tools can and can't do with your files\n\n**They can:**\n- Pull out and read the text in documents.\n- Read tables from spreadsheets into a usable form, imperfectly.\n- Read text in images, using OCR.\n- Answer questions based on what you gave them.\n- Summarise, compare and reorganise your material.\n- In the coding products, work across a whole software project.\n\n**They can't, reliably, in 2026:**\n- **Keep perfect track of every number in a huge spreadsheet.** They predict words, they don't calculate. For maths, ask for a formula or a small program, then check it.\n- **Keep the exact look of your documents.** Layout gets lost when files become text.\n- **Read diagrams and floor plans the way someone in your trade does.**\n- **Know which of two conflicting documents is current,** unless you tell them.\n- **Remember anything between chats,** except through the long-term features above. (\"Memory\" features store notes about you as text; that's Document 9.)\n- **Guarantee your file was never looked at by the vendor's safety systems** under their policies. That's why choosing the right account tier matters more than any setting inside the chat.\n\n## File formats that work well, and formats that fight back\n\n**Work well:** plain text, clean Word documents, PDFs made by software (not photographed), CSV exports from spreadsheets, and screenshots with clear text.\n\n**Fight back:**\n- PDFs with several columns (the text can get shuffled between columns).\n- Low-quality scans.\n- Complex Excel files with merged cells, formulas and several sheets. Export just the data you need as a CSV instead.\n- PowerPoint decks that are mostly pictures.\n- Anything password-protected.\n\nWhen a file fights back, copy the relevant text out and paste it. Boring, effective, always works.\n\n## The pre-session checklist\n\nFive minutes before a working session with an AI tool. Print it, and live by it.\n\n1. **Objective in one sentence.** What do I want to walk away with? \"A draft quote email\", not \"some help with the Johnson job\".\n2. **Gather the material.** The documents, numbers, emails and policies the task actually needs. If the tool has to guess, it will invent.\n3. **Sensitivity screen.** Personal information? Secrets? Under NDA? If yes: business tier, Microsoft or Google work account, or local tool. If unsure, remove the sensitive parts first: names to initials, prices to placeholders you swap back later.\n4. **Pick how long it should stay.** One-off task: paste or attach to a fresh chat. Recurring task: check the right Project or Gem exists and has current files in it. Delete or replace out-of-date files in that space. An old price list in the library produces confidently wrong quotes.\n5. **Write the request in four parts.** Role, task, material, constraints (length, tone, format, what to avoid, what to do when unsure). Document 4 has the patterns.\n6. **Decide what you'll check.** Every name, number, date, price and quoted policy. Write \"[VERIFY]\" instructions into the request if it helps.\n7. **Fresh chat for a fresh task.** Long threads get worse as the desk overflows. Start a new chat, and let the Project carry the background material.\n\n## After the session\n\n- **Save the useful output somewhere you own,** such as your documents folder. Chat history isn't a filing system.\n- **Fill the gap.** If the session showed something missing from your standing material (say, the tool needed your refund policy and no Project had it), add that file to the Project now.\n- **If anything sensitive was pasted by mistake,** follow Document 10's first checklist the same day."}, {"slug": "doc-03-shadow-ai-policy", "sourceFile": "09-doc-03-shadow-ai-policy.md", "title": "Document 3: AI Use Policy (Plain English)", "subtitle": "", "md": "**[Business Name]. For every employee, contractor and volunteer.**\n**Owner of this policy: [Name, role]. Questions and reports go to: [email / phone].**\n**Version [X], [date]. Reviewed quarterly.**\n\n*Hand this to every person who works here. Read it once properly and keep it somewhere you can find it. It's short on purpose, because anything longer doesn't get read.*\n\n---\n\n## The policy in one breath\n\nUse AI tools, but use the approved ones with your work account. Never paste passwords, personal details in bulk, or anything confidential. Check everything before it leaves the business. If something goes wrong, tell [Name] the same day, and nobody gets in trouble for reporting.\n\n## 1. Where we stand\n\nWe want you using AI tools. They save time and improve drafts, and pretending they don't exist would be like banning email in 1998.\n\nWhat we can't have is that use happening out of sight, with client data, on free personal accounts, with nobody able to answer for it. This policy brings AI use into the open. It isn't a ban.\n\nUsing AI at work is not a disciplinary matter. Hiding a mistake is harder to forgive than making one.\n\n## 2. What \"shadow AI\" means\n\nThink of using your own ute for a work delivery without telling anyone. You're being helpful, but the business doesn't know, and the insurance doesn't cover it.\n\n**Shadow AI** is the same thing with AI tools: using them for work without the business knowing. Your personal ChatGPT account on your phone. A free chatbot a friend recommended. An AI feature tucked inside some app you installed.\n\nBeing resourceful isn't the problem. The problems are that free consumer tools may keep your data, may use it to improve their models unless a setting says otherwise, sit on overseas computers with no contract protecting us, and produce output nobody checked that ends up in front of a customer.\n\n**If you're already using something we haven't approved: tell [Name]. There's no penalty for owning up. We'd much rather make a useful habit official than discover it during an incident.**\n\n## 3. Approved tools\n\nThese are the tools we approve and pay for, on business accounts with contract protections for our data:\n\n- [Tool 1, e.g. Microsoft 365 Copilot (sign in with your work account only)]\n- [Tool 2, e.g. ChatGPT Business (accounts issued by us; do not use your personal account for work)]\n- [Tool 3, e.g. our local/internal assistant (runs in the office; nothing leaves the building)]\n\nAnything not on this list needs a two-minute chat with [Name] first. The list is reviewed quarterly.\n\n## 4. What you may put into an approved AI tool\n\n- General drafting: emails, social posts, letters, meeting summaries, job descriptions.\n- Material that's already public, or that has no personal information and no secrets in it.\n- Business documents covered by the tool's account tier. (Everything on the approved list is on a tier where our data isn't used to train the vendor's models.)\n- Client material, but only if: the tool is on the approved list, you paste the minimum needed, and you follow any client-specific rules. Some contracts restrict AI use; [Name] keeps the list.\n\n## 5. What you must never put into any AI tool\n\n- **Passwords, access keys, logins, payment card numbers.** Never, into anything, ever.\n- **Full client lists, or personal information in bulk** (names with contact details, dates of birth, Medicare or tax file numbers, driver's licence numbers), unless [Name] has approved that exact use on a contracted tool.\n- **Health or medical information about any person,** unless we're a health provider and [Name] has approved a compliant tool in writing.\n- **Anything under a non-disclosure agreement or client confidentiality,** unless that agreement allows it or [Name] says so.\n- **Employee complaints, disciplinary matters, payroll details:** approved tools only, the minimum needed, and tell [Name] you're doing it.\n- **Financial figures that aren't public yet** (results before they're announced, tender pricing): approved tools only.\n\n**When in doubt, blank it out.** Write \"Client A in Toowoomba\" instead of the name, and \"[amount]\" instead of the figure. Put the real details back into the draft afterwards, in your own software.\n\n## 6. Checking the output: the part that's never optional\n\nAI tools are like a confident new starter who never says \"I don't know\". They write fluently and can be wrong with total confidence. They invent prices, dates, rules, references and quotes. So:\n\n- **You're responsible for anything you send, file or publish,** whether a person or a machine drafted it. \"The AI said so\" isn't a defence, with us or with a court.\n- Check every name, number, date, price, deadline and quoted rule against a source you trust before it leaves the business.\n- Anything going to a client, a regulator or the public gets a second pair of eyes: yours, plus the usual approver.\n- Never present AI output as checked professional advice (legal, accounting, medical, engineering) unless a qualified person here has actually checked it.\n\n## 7. If you've pasted something sensitive: same-day steps, no drama\n\n1. **Stop and write down what happened.** Which tool, which account, roughly what data, what time.\n2. **Tell [Name] the same day.** Reporting on the day is always fine. It's discoveries later on that cause problems.\n3. **Delete the chat** in the tool. If the tool has an \"exclude from training\" or temporary-chat setting, check whether it was on. (It should have been; say so if it wasn't.)\n4. **[Name] assesses it** against our privacy obligations: was it personal information, could it cause serious harm, do we have to report it? Usually the answer is \"contained, logged, move on\". Sometimes there's more to do, and doing it fast is what keeps it small.\n5. **We log it** in the incident register: one line, no names, no blame. The log exists to fix patterns, not people.\n\n## 8. Clients, honesty and disclosure\n\nIf a client asks whether AI was used on their work, answer honestly and without embarrassment: \"We use AI tools for drafting and summarising, on contracted business platforms, and a qualified person checks everything before it reaches you. Here's our policy.\"\n\nIf a client's contract restricts AI use, we follow the contract. We never imply a person did work that a machine did unsupervised, and we never claim checks we didn't do.\n\n## 9. Personal use on work devices\n\nUsing AI for your own things, in your own time, on your own account is your business, with two limits: not on systems that hold client data, and never pasting work material into personal accounts. The line is the data, not the device.\n\n## 10. Who to talk to\n\n- **Tool questions, approvals, new-tool requests:** [Name], [contact].\n- **\"I think I pasted something I shouldn't have\":** [Name], same day, any hour, no penalty for reporting.\n- **Client contract questions about AI:** [Name], before you start the work, not after.\n- **This policy is reviewed every quarter** against the tools we use and the rules that apply to us. Suggestions are genuinely welcome; the best policies are the ones staff improve.\n\n---\n\n*Acknowledgement: I have read and understood this policy. Name: ______ Signed: ______ Date: ______*"}, {"slug": "doc-04-prompting-cheat-sheet", "sourceFile": "10-doc-04-prompting-cheat-sheet.md", "title": "Document 4: Prompting Cheat Sheet for Daily Tasks", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack. One page, pin it up.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, and any local model chat (Ollama, LM Studio).\n**Version:** October 2026.\n\n**The universal pattern:** Role + Task + Material + Constraints. It's the job sheet you'd hand an apprentice: who it's for, what to do, what to use, and the standard to meet. Give it the ingredients and the limits, and the tool has less room to guess.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Drafting emails\n\n*Why it works: the facts and the goal are on the page, so the tool doesn't have to invent them.*\n\n**Pattern A (the situation):**\n> Draft an email to [name] at [business]. Situation: [facts, dates, amounts]. Goal: [what you want to happen]. Tone: [friendly/firm]. Max [X] words. Don't apologise unless the facts warrant it. End with a clear next step.\n\n**Pattern B (the reply):**\n> Here is the email I received: [paste]. Draft a reply that: [answers their question / declines the request / buys time until Friday]. Keep my usual tone: [brief, warm, Australian]. No corporate phrases. Max 100 words.\n\n*Platform notes:* all four major platforms do this well. Copilot inside Outlook drafts in place using the email thread, which is fastest if your business runs on Microsoft. Gemini inside Gmail does the same for Google businesses. ChatGPT and Claude are best when you paste the thread in and want tighter control of tone.\n\n## 2. Summarising meeting notes\n\n*Why it works: \"do not add anything\" and \"[UNCLEAR]\" give the tool permission to leave gaps instead of filling them with invention.*\n\n**Pattern A (rough to readable):**\n> These are my rough notes from [meeting, date, attendees]: [paste]. Rewrite them as clean minutes: decisions made, action items with owner and due date, and open questions. Keep it under one page. Do not add anything I did not write. If an owner or date is missing, mark it [UNCLEAR].\n\n**Pattern B (the long meeting):**\n> Here is the transcript/notes from a [X]-minute meeting: [paste or attach]. Give me: (1) a five-sentence summary, (2) every decision, (3) every action item as \"who / what / by when\", (4) anything that was raised but parked. Quote exact figures only where they appear in the notes.\n\n*Platform notes:* Claude and ChatGPT handle long, rough material well. NotebookLM (Google) is excellent when the notes are in Drive and you want answers strictly from your own documents. Copilot in Teams can summarise the meeting itself if your organisation has it switched on, so you don't paste anything. Check names and numbers every time: note-takers mumble, and models smooth over gaps by inventing.\n\n## 3. First-draft reports\n\n*Why it works: [VERIFY] marks and the gap list make the tool show you where it's guessing.*\n\n**Pattern A:**\n> Draft a [report type] for [audience]. Purpose: [decision it supports]. Key facts and figures: [paste or attach source material]. Structure: executive summary (max 150 words), then [sections]. Plain Australian English. Mark every figure you did not get from my material as [VERIFY].\n\n**Pattern B (from existing documents):**\n> Using only the attached documents [attach], write a draft [report type] answering: [the question]. Cite which document each major point came from. End with a list of gaps: what the question needs that these documents do not cover.\n\n*Platform notes:* Claude Projects and ChatGPT Projects are the right home for recurring reports, because the standing material lives there. Gemini and NotebookLM shine when the sources are in Google Drive. Copilot drafts directly into Word with your templates. Pattern B's \"list the gaps\" step is the best defence against a confident report built on thin sources, which is how the Deloitte case from Class 2 happens at your scale.\n\n## 4. Brainstorming\n\n*Why it works: limits and categories stop it giving you fifteen versions of the same safe idea.*\n\n**Pattern A:**\n> I need ideas for [goal, e.g. getting more commercial maintenance contracts]. Constraints: [budget, region, team size, what we will not do]. Give me 15 ideas: 5 safe, 5 bold, 5 cheap-and-fast. One sentence each, then mark your top 3 with a reason.\n\n**Pattern B (the thinking partner):**\n> Let's work through [problem]. Ask me one question at a time to understand the situation before suggesting anything. After five questions, propose three approaches with the trade-offs of each.\n\n*Platform notes:* all platforms are fine here; none is magical. Pattern B turns the chatbot into an interviewer, which reliably produces better thinking than \"give me ideas\". Treat the results as prompts for your own judgement, not answers. The model recombines what's common, so its \"bold\" ideas are usually only medium-bold.\n\n## 5. Plain language, both ways\n\n*Why it works: if something can't be said simply, you've found the part nobody has really explained yet.*\n\n**Pattern A (make it plain for someone else):**\n> Rewrite this for [audience, e.g. a customer with no construction knowledge]: [paste]. Plain Australian English, short sentences, define any term you must keep. Keep every number and deadline exactly as given.\n\n**Pattern B (explain it to me):**\n> Explain this to me like I'm smart but have never worked in [field]: [paste the contract clause / council notice / insurance letter]. What does it actually require me to do? What are the traps? Quote the exact phrases that matter.\n\n**Pattern C (check I've understood it):** This is the Feynman Technique from the course, with the tool as your listener.\n> I'm going to explain [topic or document] back to you in my own words, as if to a new staff member. Point out anything I got wrong, anything important I left out, and anywhere I used jargon without explaining it. Don't rewrite my explanation; just list the problems. Here's my explanation: [your words].\n\n*Platform notes:* Claude tends to be strongest at careful plain-language rewriting; ChatGPT and Gemini are both good; Copilot is good inside your own documents. Pattern B is superb on legal and insurance text, with one hard rule: it tells you what the document seems to say, and a qualified person tells you what it means for you.\n\n## 6. Checking your own work\n\n*Why it works: the tool is better at comparing two texts it can see than at knowing facts it can't.*\n\n**Pattern A (the cold reader):**\n> Read this as a sceptical [customer / auditor / council officer]: [paste]. List: (1) anything unclear, (2) anything that sounds like a promise we might not want to make, (3) any inconsistency in numbers or dates, (4) typos. Be blunt. Do not rewrite it yet.\n\n**Pattern B (the fact-check pass):**\n> Here is a draft: [paste]. Here are the source facts: [paste/attach]. Check every name, number, date, price and claim in the draft against the sources. Output a table: claim / supported by source? / what's wrong. Do not fix anything, just report.\n\n*Platform notes:* Pattern B is the most valuable habit on this sheet, and it works on every platform. One honest limit: the model is good at checking your draft against your sources, but it can't reliably check either against the real world. If a fact matters and it didn't come from material you supplied, check it yourself (or with a tool that does live web search, then check anyway).\n\n---\n\n## The bottom strip\n\n- One task per chat. Fresh chat, fresh desk.\n- Paste the real material. No material, no accuracy.\n- Limits are a kindness to future-you: length, tone, format, \"if unsure, ask\".\n- Every name, number, date and quoted rule gets checked by a person. That person is you.\n- Sensitive data: business-tier or local tools only (see Documents 3 and 5)."}, {"slug": "doc-05-data-security-basics", "sourceFile": "11-doc-05-data-security-basics.md", "title": "Document 5: Data Security Basics for AI Tools", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini and Google Workspace (Google), Copilot and Microsoft 365 (Microsoft), and local tools (Ollama, LM Studio with Llama, Mistral, Qwen or similar).\n**Written for:** someone who has never read a privacy policy. Everything here assumes zero background.\n**Last verified: 13 October 2026.** Settings menus move and policies get updated. Re-check quarterly; the review box at the end tells you how.\n\n---\n\n## In plain terms\n\nTyping into a chatbot sends your words to a company's computers, where copies are kept. Free accounts may use what you type to improve their products; paid business accounts promise in a contract not to. So: switch off the training settings today, use work accounts for work, and never paste anything you couldn't explain to the person it's about.\n\n## 1. What happens when you type into a chatbot\n\nPicture posting a letter to a company overseas. They read it, write back, and keep a copy on file.\n\nStep by step:\n\n1. **You type and press enter.** Your message goes over the internet to the company's computers, usually in the United States. It travels encrypted, which means scrambled so casual eavesdroppers can't read it.\n2. **The model reads it.** The model (the very large stack of numbers from Class 1) reads your message together with the rest of the conversation, and writes a reply that comes back to your screen.\n3. **Copies are kept.** Your message is usually:\n   - **stored in your account history,** so you can scroll back;\n   - **logged on the company's systems,** for running the service, safety checks and, depending on your settings and plan, possibly improving the model;\n   - **possibly read by company staff or contractors** under their review policies, for example when a safety system flags a conversation.\n4. **Deleting isn't erasing.** Deleting the chat removes it from your view and starts the company's deletion process. It doesn't mean the text never existed on their systems.\n\nTreat chats on free consumer accounts as permanent. Treat chats on business accounts as governed by a contract: much better, but the data still left your building.\n\n**With a local tool** (Ollama, LM Studio), none of this happens. The model runs on your own computer and your text goes nowhere. The trade-off is that updates, security and maintenance are your job.\n\n## 2. \"Training on your data\", in plain words\n\nThink of a teacher marking thousands of essays. Reading them slowly shapes how the teacher teaches next year. Your essay played a small part, but the teacher can't hand it to another student, because it isn't kept in a filing cabinet for anyone to open.\n\n**Training** means adjusting a model's numbers using text, so future versions behave differently. If a company trains on your data, your prompts and files can become part of what shapes the next model.\n\n**What it doesn't mean:** the model doesn't become a database someone can search for your client list. A model repeating a specific small business's documents word for word is very unlikely.\n\n**What it does mean, in practice:**\n- your business information sits in the company's systems for far longer;\n- human reviewers may read it as part of quality and safety work;\n- it's exposed if the company is ever hacked;\n- it may subtly influence a product your competitors also use.\n\nFor most businesses, the everyday risks are how long data is kept, who reads it, and breaches, more than training itself. But the training setting usually decides how long and how widely your data is kept, which is why the opt-outs below matter.\n\n## 3. Opt-outs and defaults, platform by platform (step by step)\n\n**ChatGPT (OpenAI).** Consumer Free/Plus: conversations may be used to improve models unless you opt out. Steps: profile icon, Settings, Data controls, turn off \"Improve the model for everyone.\" For one-off sensitive chats use Temporary Chat (it is not used for training and disappears from history). Pro plan: not used for training. Business and Enterprise plans: business data excluded from training by default, with contractual commitments and admin controls. Retention after deletion on consumer plans is generally around 30 days and longer for data selected for training or flagged for review.\n\n**Claude (Anthropic).** Consumer Free/Pro: chats are retained for about 30 days by default. Under the consumer terms updated in August 2025, using your chats for training is an opt-in: you are asked to choose, and if you allow it, retention extends to up to five years. Steps: Settings, Privacy, and make sure the \"use my data for training\" style choice is off. If you never saw a prompt, go looking in Privacy settings anyway. Team and Enterprise plans: your content is not used to train models.\n\n**Gemini (Google).** Consumer Gemini app: your prompts are saved to your Google Account activity and may be used to improve Google products only if \"Gemini Apps Activity\" is on. Steps: gemini.google.com, Settings, Gemini Apps Activity, switch it off (this also stops the human-review pipeline for saved prompts; note Google may still keep some data for other purposes such as abuse protection). Google Workspace (paid business plans): prompts and files in Gemini features are not used to train models outside your organisation by default, and Workspace data protections apply. NotebookLM: works with sources you upload; check the same activity settings apply to your account type.\n\n**Copilot (Microsoft).** Consumer Copilot (personal Microsoft account): chats may be used to improve Microsoft's products and services unless you opt out. Steps: Microsoft account privacy dashboard (privacy.microsoft.com), manage product and service improvement settings, and in the Copilot app settings turn off conversation data sharing where offered. Work or school account (Microsoft 365 Copilot or Copilot Chat signed in with your organisational account): prompts and responses are not used to train foundation models, covered by Microsoft's commercial data protection and Data Protection Addendum.\n\n**The most important point on this page:** the same-looking Copilot app behaves completely differently depending on which account you're signed into. Personal account: personal rules. Work account: work protections. Check the account before you paste.\n\n**Local (Ollama, LM Studio, Llama and other open models).** Nothing to opt out of; your data never leaves the machine. Your security job moves to the machine itself:\n- keep the operating system updated;\n- don't make a local AI server reachable from the internet without professional help;\n- remember that anyone who can use the computer can see the chats.\n\n## 4. What a Data Processing Agreement (DPA) is, and when you need one\n\nThink of the register a business keeps of who holds keys to the building: who has which key, which doors it opens, and when they hand it back.\n\nA **DPA** is that register for personal information. It's the contract between you and a software company that says what they may do with personal information you give them:\n\n- use it only for the service you're paying for;\n- keep it secure, to a defined standard;\n- tell you if there's a breach;\n- delete it when you leave;\n- follow limits on any subcontractors.\n\n**You need one** whenever your business puts personal information into someone else's software. Personal information is anything that identifies a person: names with contact details, health information, employment records, an individual's financial details.\n\nBusiness and enterprise tiers of all four major platforms include a DPA automatically, and that's a large part of what the higher price actually buys. Consumer accounts offer no DPA and nothing equivalent. That's why \"the free account is fine for work\" is the sentence that starts most AI data incidents.\n\n## 5. Never put these into a consumer AI tool\n\n- Passwords, access keys, login details, payment card numbers. Into any AI tool, ever, business tier included wherever you can avoid it.\n- Client lists or personal information in bulk (names plus contact details, dates of birth, tax file or Medicare numbers).\n- Client financial data: bank details, financial statements under NDA, tax records of identifiable people.\n- Health or medical information about any identifiable person.\n- Anything covered by an NDA or professional confidentiality, unless the tool's business-tier contract covers it and the NDA allows it.\n- Employee disciplinary or grievance material. Not on consumer tools at all; on contracted tools, only the minimum needed.\n- Financial results that aren't public yet, tender pricing, or news before it's announced.\n\n**If the task genuinely needs that material,** use the business tier with the DPA, your Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace tool signed in with a work account, or a local model. Or blank out the sensitive parts first and put them back afterwards in your own software.\n\n## 6. The four habits that do most of the work\n\n1. **Work data on work accounts.** Personal accounts stay personal. Check which account you're signed into before pasting, every time; the icons look alike.\n2. **The decision drill.** Before pasting: personal information? Secrets? Under NDA? If any answer is yes, change tool or blank it out.\n3. **Settings once, then every quarter.** Do the opt-outs above today, and diary a quarterly re-check in the same slot as your Document 6 pricing review.\n4. **Report the same day.** If anything sensitive went where it shouldn't, tell the policy owner that day (Document 3, section 7; Document 10, scenario 1). Reported fast, an incident is a line in a log. Discovered late, it's a crisis.\n\n## 7. Quarterly review box\n\n**Next review due: [date].**\n\nOn review day, for each tool in use:\n\n1. Sign in and open the privacy or data settings.\n2. Confirm the training and retention settings are still as this document describes.\n3. Check the vendor's privacy page for changes (each vendor shows a \"last updated\" date).\n4. Record anything that changed in the tool register.\n\nTen minutes per tool. This document and Document 6 go out of date fastest, which is why both carry dates.\n\n## Australian context, in one paragraph\n\nIf your organisation handles personal information and is covered by the Privacy Act (most organisations over $3 million turnover, plus many smaller ones in health, personal-information trading and Commonwealth contracting), then: you stay responsible for that information when it goes to an overseas AI vendor (APP 8); you must take reasonable steps to protect it (APP 11), and business tiers plus a written policy are exactly such steps; an eligible data breach at your vendor can trigger your duty to notify the OAIC and the affected people; and from 10 December 2026, your privacy policy must describe significant automated decision-making about people. The OAIC's generative AI guidance boils down to four words: minimise, secure, be transparent, keep people accountable. This document puts all four into practice. It's practical guidance, not legal advice. For your specific situation, especially with health information, talk to a lawyer who knows privacy."}, {"slug": "doc-06-tool-comparison-matrix", "sourceFile": "12-doc-06-tool-comparison-matrix.md", "title": "Document 6: AI Tool Comparison Matrix", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**LAST VERIFIED: 13 October 2026.** Pricing and plans in this industry change every few months. This matrix is a snapshot, not scripture. Re-verify quarterly (put it in the same calendar slot as the Document 5 review). Prices below are US dollars, the global standard vendors publish; Australian-dollar prices differ (typically higher, plus GST). Always confirm on the vendor's own pricing page before budgeting.\n\n---\n\n## In plain terms\n\nThe four big AI products are more alike than their marketing suggests. For most small businesses, the best choice is whichever one plugs into the email and files you already use, on a paid business account so your data is protected by contract. Add a local tool only if you handle genuinely sensitive material.\n\n## How to read this table\n\nRemember Class 1: the model is the engine, the product is the car. This table compares cars: what they cost, what they're good at, and what they do with your data. Engines get swapped regularly under the bonnet, so the \"best at\" row describes each car's strengths today, not a permanent ranking.\n\nRead the rows in this order: **Trains on your data?** first, then **Business/team** pricing, then **Best at**. The first two decide whether a product is safe and affordable for work; the third is a tiebreaker.\n\n## The matrix\n\n| | **ChatGPT (OpenAI)** | **Claude (Anthropic)** | **Gemini (Google)** | **Copilot (Microsoft)** | **Llama (Meta) local** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Free tier** | Yes, generous, rate-limited | Yes, rate-limited | Yes (consumer app, with a Google account) | Yes (consumer Copilot app/web) | The models are free; your hardware is not |\n| **Individual paid** | Go ~US$8/mo (limited markets); Plus US$20/mo (~A$31); Pro US$200/mo | Pro US$17/mo annual, US$20 monthly; Max US$100–200/mo | Google AI Pro ~US$19.99/mo; AI Ultra US$249.99/mo (heavy users only) | Copilot Pro ~US$20/mo (personal Microsoft 365 users) | $0 software; a usable 8B-class setup runs on a 16GB laptop; 12–32B models want 24–32GB memory or a 12GB+ graphics card |\n| **Business/team** | Business (formerly Team) ~US$25–30/user/mo (annual vs monthly), low seat minimum | Team ~US$25/seat/mo annual (~US$30 monthly), typically 5-seat minimum; Premium tier (~US$150) adds Claude Code allowances | Bundled into Workspace plans: Starter ~US$7, Standard ~US$14, Plus ~US$22–26/user/mo; Gemini Enterprise sold separately for larger deployments | Microsoft 365 Copilot Business ~US$21/user/mo annual commitment (Microsoft's business page also lists a US$32 \"Premium with Copilot\" tier; check which applies); Copilot Chat is included free with M365 business subscriptions (agents metered) | Free Community Licence for virtually all SMBs (licence obligations kick in only at hyperscale); Meta provides no support, you own everything |\n| **Enterprise** | Quote-only; third-party procurement reports converge around US$45–75/seat/mo | Quote-only (Team Enterprise tier ~US$20–125/seat depending on structure reported in 2026; get a quote) | Workspace Enterprise quote-only | ~US$30/user/mo annual with qualifying M365 plan | Self-hosted; your IT problem, your control |\n| **Best at** | The all-rounder: ecosystem (Projects, GPTs, memory, canvas, voice, image generation), broad task coverage, largest third-party integration world | Long documents and careful writing; Projects with persistent files; generally cautious, well-behaved output; strong coding tools (Claude Code) | If you live in Google Workspace: inside Gmail, Docs, Meet, Drive; NotebookLM for working strictly within your own documents; bundled, so no extra purchase | If you live in Microsoft 365: inside Outlook, Word, Excel, Teams with your existing permissions; work-account data protection out of the box | Total privacy: nothing leaves the building; offline use; no per-seat fees; customisable |\n| **Weak at** | Consumer tier trains on your data unless you opt out; feature sprawl can confuse; price climbs fast with add-ons | Smaller ecosystem and fewer integrations than OpenAI/Microsoft; consumer plan's training opt-in comes with 5-year retention if accepted | Consumer activity settings are a maze (see Doc 5); Workspace value depends on already paying for Workspace | Consumer Copilot and work Copilot look identical but behave differently, which trips people up; best features assume M365 subscriptions | Requires someone technical to own it; smaller models trail frontier cloud models on hard reasoning; no built-in ecosystem (your Drive/SharePoint do not appear inside it) |\n| **Trains on your data?** | Free/Plus: yes unless you opt out. Pro: no. Business/Enterprise: no (contractual) | Free/Pro: only if you opt in (default/consent prompted since Aug 2025; opt-in extends retention to 5 years). Team/Enterprise: no | Consumer: only if Gemini Apps Activity is on. Workspace business: not outside your domain | Consumer: may be used to improve services unless opted out. Work/school account: not used to train foundation models (contractual) | Nothing is transmitted anywhere; no training happens on your chats unless you do it yourself |\n| **Business DPA / admin controls** | Yes on Business/Enterprise | Yes on Team/Enterprise | Yes via Workspace terms | Yes, strongest of the group via Microsoft DPA | Not applicable (you are the processor) |\n\n## How to choose: two questions\n\n**Question 1: what does your business already run on?**\n- Email, files and calendar in **Microsoft 365**: Copilot is the easiest path and the best governed, because it already knows who's allowed to see what.\n- Email, files and calendar in **Google Workspace**: Gemini, for the same reason.\n- **Neither:** question 2 decides.\n\n**Question 2: what's your main task?**\n- Long documents, drafting and careful rewriting: **Claude**.\n- A broad mix (voice, images, custom helpers, lots of connections to other apps): **ChatGPT**.\n- Strict privacy needs or lots of NDA work: a **local Llama-class setup**, or as the practical middle, a business tier of either cloud product with its contract protections.\n\n**The common-sense 2026 setup for a ten-person business with no strong ecosystem:** two or three seats on one cloud product's business tier (ChatGPT Business or Claude Team, roughly US$25–30 per user per month) for day-to-day work. Optionally, add one capable computer running Ollama or LM Studio with a mid-size open model for the sensitive material. Total: under A$150 per month all in. The alternative, everyone on free personal accounts with client data, costs nothing and risks everything.\n\n## What changes fastest (re-check these quarterly)\n\n- **Prices and plan names.** OpenAI renamed Team to Business in 2025; expect more renaming.\n- **Free-tier limits.**\n- **Which model sits under each product.** The engine gets swapped regularly; the car keeps its name.\n- **Data-handling defaults.** Anthropic's consumer terms changed significantly in August 2025, and Google's activity settings have moved several times.\n- **Where tiers are sold.** For example, which countries get ChatGPT Go.\n- **The gap between the best cloud models and the best local models.** It narrows every year."}, {"slug": "doc-07-meeting-email-workflow", "sourceFile": "13-doc-07-meeting-email-workflow.md", "title": "Document 7: Meeting and Email Workflow Guide", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** Microsoft 365 Copilot (Outlook, Teams, Word), Gemini in Google Workspace (Gmail, Meet, Docs) and NotebookLM, ChatGPT, Claude, and any local tool. The workflow is the same everywhere; the notes say which tool suits which step.\n**Version:** October 2026.\n\n---\n\n## In plain terms\n\nTreat the AI tool as a very fast junior minute-taker. It can turn your scrappy notes into tidy minutes, an action list and follow-up emails in minutes. But it wasn't really in the room, so you check every name, number, date and promise before anything goes out.\n\n## The five steps\n\nPrep, capture, clean, extract, follow up. Run them the same way every time and it stops being \"using AI\" and becomes \"how we do meetings\".\n\nSteps marked **[HUMAN]** are where a person must make the call. They're not fine print; they're the process. The junior does the typing. You stay the chair.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Prep (10 minutes before the meeting)\n\n**The prompt:**\n> I have a meeting at [time] with [who, their role/relationship]. Purpose: [e.g. quarterly review of the cleaning contract]. Here is the agenda: [paste]. Here are my notes from last time: [paste]. Give me: (1) a five-bullet briefing on where things stand, (2) the three questions I should make sure get answered, (3) any obvious gaps in the agenda.\n\n**Tool notes.**\n- **Microsoft 365 Copilot** is strongest here if your history lives in Outlook and Teams. Ask it directly (\"Prepare me for my 2pm with Bayside Dental using our past emails and the last meeting recap\") and it searches your mail and calendar, seeing only what you're allowed to see.\n- **Gemini** does the same across Gmail and Calendar in Workspace.\n- **ChatGPT or Claude** work well with pasted material. They're the right choice for sensitive commercial meetings if you're on their business tiers, as is a local tool.\n\n**[HUMAN]** You decide the three questions that actually matter. The tool's suggestions are a checklist, not the strategy.\n\n## Step 2: Capture (during the meeting)\n\n**Option A, automatic.** Teams (with Copilot) and Google Meet (with Gemini, on eligible Workspace plans) can record and transcribe the meeting. Only switch it on with everyone's knowledge. Australian recording laws require the consent of the people being recorded, and \"the AI was listening\" is not a conversation to have afterwards.\n\n**Option B, manual (works anywhere, including the café).** Keep a scratch document open and type rough notes, with no care for spelling or punctuation. What matters: names, numbers, decisions, and who said what. Don't try to write proper sentences during a meeting; that's Step 3's job. Dictating into your phone's notes app works too.\n\n**[HUMAN]** Nothing leaves the room yet. Raw notes may contain offhand comments, and Step 3's edit is where those come out.\n\n## Step 3: Clean (within a day, while memory is fresh)\n\n**The prompt:**\n> These are rough notes from [meeting, date, attendees]: [paste]. Rewrite as clean minutes: short opening line (purpose, attendees, date), then Decisions, then Discussion points (three to six bullets), then Open questions. Use only what is in the notes. Remove side comments and anything that reads as a personal remark about an attendee. Keep it under one page. Mark anything ambiguous [UNCLEAR].\n\n**Tool notes.**\n- **Claude and ChatGPT** both do this well from pasted notes.\n- **Copilot in Word, or in Teams beside the transcript,** keeps everything inside Microsoft. That matters if your business runs on it, because work-account content isn't used to train the underlying models.\n- **Gemini in Docs, or NotebookLM** with the transcript as a source, is the Google equivalent.\n- If the transcript came from Teams or Meet, Copilot or Gemini can summarise it directly and you skip the paste.\n\n**[HUMAN]** Read the clean minutes in full before they go anywhere. Check every name, number and date, and sort out the [UNCLEAR] items yourself. You were in the room; the tool wasn't.\n\n## Step 4: Extract actions\n\n**The prompt:**\n> From these minutes: [paste cleaned version]. Produce an action list as a table: Action / Owner / Due date / Source (which decision or discussion point it came from). If an owner or date is not stated in the minutes, write [ASSIGN] or [DATE?] instead of inventing one. Then list anything raised in discussion that has no action and no decision attached, so I can decide if it needs one.\n\n**Tool notes.** Any platform handles this; it's just reorganising. The real value is the last sentence: the list of \"orphan\" items catches exactly the things meetings lose. If actions go into a task app (Planner, Asana, Trello and similar all have AI features now), ask the tool to format the list the way you enter tasks there.\n\n**[HUMAN]** Confirm owners and dates with the actual people before the list goes out. An action assigned by a machine to someone who never agreed to it is how trust in the whole system dies in week two.\n\n## Step 5: Follow-up emails (same day or next morning)\n\n**The prompt (one per recipient, or one combined):**\n> Draft a follow-up email to [name] from [meeting]. Tone: [warm and brief / formal]. Include: thanks, the two or three decisions relevant to them, their action items with dates, and anything I owe them with my dates. Here are the minutes: [paste]. Max 120 words. Nothing that is not in the minutes.\n\n**Tool notes.**\n- **Copilot inside Outlook** drafts directly in your mail with the thread and calendar in view. Smoothest if you run on Microsoft.\n- **Gemini's \"Help me write\" in Gmail** is the Google equivalent.\n- Otherwise, paste into **ChatGPT or Claude** and copy the result back.\n- For recurring follow-ups (same client, same weekly meeting), save the prompt in your Project, Gem or custom instructions so Step 5 takes two clicks.\n\n**[HUMAN]** A final read before sending. Every time, no exceptions, forever. Check every promise the email makes on your behalf: dates you can hit, prices that are current, commitments that were actually agreed. The email has your name on it. In the Air Canada case from Class 2, the company's name on the chatbot's words was enough to make the company liable.\n\n---\n\n## The weekly rhythm, condensed\n\n| When | What | Tool | Human check |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10 min before | Briefing and three questions | Copilot / Gemini in your ecosystem, or ChatGPT/Claude with pasted notes | You pick the real questions |\n| During | Rough capture or consented transcript | Teams/Meet transcription, or your scratch doc | Consent for recording |\n| Within a day | Clean minutes | Same ecosystem, or paste to Claude/ChatGPT | Full read; fix names, numbers, dates |\n| Same sitting | Action table plus orphan items | Any | Confirm owners and dates with people |\n| Same or next day | Follow-up emails | Outlook/Gmail in-place, or paste | Final read before send, always |\n\n## Where this workflow must not go\n\n- **Sensitive meetings** (disciplinary, mergers and acquisitions, health information, legally privileged): contracted business-tier or local tools only, or clean the notes up by hand.\n- **Client-facing minutes:** your edit is the finished product; the tool's draft is just scaffolding. Some clients' contracts restrict AI use, and your policy owner keeps track of those (Document 3, section 4).\n- **Recording:** consent first, always, in the room, out loud."}, {"slug": "doc-08-dont-panic-faq", "sourceFile": "14-doc-08-dont-panic-faq.md", "title": "Document 8: The \"Don't Panic\" FAQ", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack. For every employee, including the ones who did not ask.**\n**Version:** October 2026. Straight answers, no corporate nothing-speak.\n\n---\n\n## In plain terms\n\nAI tools are fast, confident drafting assistants. They're useful, they make mistakes, and they don't think. Use them for first drafts, keep work data on work accounts, and check anything with a name, number or date before it goes out. Everything below is a longer version of that.\n\n## 1. Is AI going to take my job?\n\n**Short answer:** parts of jobs, yes. Whole jobs like yours, mostly no, and not on the timetable the headlines suggest.\n\nAI is good at first drafts, summaries and tidying text. So the parts of your work that are routine writing and reformatting will get faster, and whoever signs your pay will expect some of that time back.\n\nWhat it can't do: turn up, take responsibility, read the room with a real customer, notice that the numbers smell wrong, or be the person someone rings when it matters.\n\nThe honest risk isn't a robot replacing you. It's a competitor using these tools to serve customers faster while you don't. That's a reason to learn the tools, not fear them.\n\nThe people most exposed are those whose whole job is producing unchecked text in volume. Even there, the failures of this decade show quality collapsing when people are removed too fast: Deloitte refunded part of a government report, and Klarna started hiring people back. Learning to use the tools well and check their work is the most durable position available.\n\n## 2. Can the AI see my screen?\n\n**Short answer:** not by default.\n\nA chatbot sees only what you send it: what you type, files you attach, images you upload, and, in the office-suite products, the emails, documents and calendar items it's been given access to on your work account.\n\nTwo real exceptions:\n- **Voice and screen-sharing features.** If you deliberately start a voice session or share your screen with the tool, it processes what you share.\n- **Office-suite assistants.** Microsoft 365 Copilot and Gemini for Workspace can read across your work email and files. That's their selling point, and it's limited by the permissions your IT admin sets.\n\nNothing is quietly screenshotting you in the background of a normal chat. The thing to watch isn't surveillance, it's what you choose to paste, which is what Documents 3 and 5 are about.\n\n## 3. If I put my client list in, does the AI company now have my client list?\n\n**Short answer:** for as long as their systems keep it, yes.\n\nTyping into a chatbot is like posting a letter to a company overseas that keeps a copy on file. What happens to that copy depends on your account:\n- **Free consumer account:** it may be kept for a while after you delete it, may be used to improve their models unless you opted out, and may be seen by their reviewers in some circumstances.\n- **Business account:** how long it's kept is set by contract, it isn't used for training, and it must be deleted when you ask or leave.\n- **Local tool:** the list never went anywhere.\n\nWhat doesn't happen is the science-fiction version: your list doesn't become searchable by other users or get sold as a database.\n\nThe practical answer is in Document 5: personal information in bulk belongs in contracted tools or local tools, and nowhere else.\n\n## 4. Why does it sometimes make stuff up?\n\n**Short answer:** because it's built to sound right, not to be right.\n\nPicture a student sitting an exam on a topic they never studied. They've read plenty of top-mark essays, so they know exactly what a good answer sounds like, and they write one. It reads beautifully. Parts of it are invented.\n\nAn AI model predicts the most plausible next words. When it hasn't seen reliable information about your exact question, it produces the shape of an answer: a confident sentence, a realistic-looking reference, a number in roughly the right range. It has no inner alarm that tells \"I know this\" apart from \"this is what that kind of sentence looks like\", so the inventions arrive with total confidence.\n\nThis is how these tools work, not a bug an update will fully remove, although newer products reduce it with web search and by sticking to documents you supply.\n\n**Your defence:** give it the real material, tell it to answer only from what you gave it, and check every name, number, date and reference before anything leaves the building.\n\n## 5. Do I need to disclose that I used AI in my work?\n\n**Short answer:** there's no general Australian law requiring it, but four situations call for disclosure or care.\n\n1. **Your contract.** Some client agreements restrict AI use. Breaching one is a commercial and legal problem, and it's exactly how Deloitte ended up refunding part of a government report.\n2. **Regulated advice.** Legal, accounting, medical and engineering work must be checked and owned by a qualified person. Presenting unchecked AI text as professional advice can breach your professional obligations, whatever the AI rules say.\n3. **Automated decisions about people.** From 10 December 2026, privacy policies must describe significant automated decision-making. If AI substantially shapes decisions about customers or employees, that has to be disclosed.\n4. **Trust.** When a client asks, answer honestly and specifically. Being caught implying a person checked something they didn't ends relationships. \"We draft with AI tools and a qualified person checks everything\" is a perfectly respectable sentence.\n\n**In one line:** always disclose when asked, and disclose up front for advice-grade work.\n\n## 6. Is my employer reading my chats with the AI?\n\n**Short answer:** on tools the business provides, assume they can.\n\n- **Work accounts** on Microsoft 365 Copilot, Google Workspace, ChatGPT Business or Claude Team let administrators manage, audit and often review usage, exactly like work email. That's part of what a business account is.\n- **Your personal account on your personal phone:** your employer generally can't see the content. On business-owned devices, monitoring software is a separate question with a separate answer.\n\nThe healthy rule is the same as for work email: nothing on a work account or work device that you wouldn't want your manager to read, and no work material on personal accounts (that's shadow AI, Document 3). If you're unsure what monitoring applies to you, ask. A legitimate employer can explain its policy in two sentences.\n\n## 7. Is it safe to let AI write emails I send to customers?\n\n**Short answer:** drafting, yes. Sending unchecked, no.\n\nAI is genuinely good at a competent first draft of a customer email, faster than you, and on routine messages the draft is often better than ours would be.\n\nThe ways it goes wrong are specific, and you can check for them:\n- it can invent a promise, such as a discount, deadline or warranty;\n- it can get a name or number wrong;\n- it can strike a tone that doesn't suit your relationship with that customer.\n\nAir Canada learned the hard way that a business owns what its AI tells customers. So: one read-through before sending, checking promises, figures and names. Keep a person involved in anything consequential, such as complaints, money, cancellations, or anything a lawyer might one day read aloud. Treat the tool as a fast typist, not your account manager.\n\n## 8. Will these tools keep getting smarter until they run everything?\n\n**Short answer:** they'll keep improving. Nobody knows how far or how fast, and anyone quoting a confident date is selling something.\n\nWhat we can see is that progress comes in jumps on some tasks (coding, document work) and stalls on others (reliability, long-range judgement, the last mile of checking).\n\nEven a much smarter tool stays a tool in the way that matters to you. Someone has to decide the goal, supply the background, own the result and answer for the consequences. Businesses, professions and the law will keep requiring a responsible person at that point.\n\nFor your working life, take the attitude every trade took to power tools: learn it, respect what it can do, keep your hand on the switch. The people displaced by power drills weren't the ones who picked them up.\n\n## 9. What can I actually trust it to do today without checking every line?\n\n**Short answer:** low-stakes jobs where you'll see the result anyway.\n\nReformatting, summarising for your own eyes, brainstorming, turning jargon into plain language, and first drafts you're going to rewrite.\n\nAnything with a number, a name, a date, a legal rule or a customer attached needs the check. The check is usually faster than doing the task from scratch, which is why the honest claim is \"faster with a review\", not \"done for you\".\n\n**A good test:** if this output were wrong, would anyone be harmed, embarrassed or out of pocket? If yes, a person reads it. If no, go for it and enjoy the time saved. Applied consistently, that test is a complete personal AI policy.\n\n## 10. Everyone at work is using it and I'm behind. How do I catch up this week?\n\n**Short answer:** one hour, spread over three evenings.\n\n- **Evening one:** set up an account on the tool your workplace approves. (Ask who decides; if nobody knows, that's your Document 3 conversation.) Paste in the Document 1 template for your type of business, and run one real task through it: tomorrow's email, or today's messy notes.\n- **Evening two:** take the cheat sheet (Document 4) and do one task from each of its six sections on real work, noticing which two are worth keeping.\n- **Evening three:** do the settings check from Document 5 on your account, then set up one Project or Gem with your three most-used reference documents in it.\n\nAfter that, the habit is the skill. Use it once a day on something you were going to do anyway, and check its work the way you'd check a keen apprentice's, because that's exactly what it is."}, {"slug": "doc-09-custom-instructions-memory", "sourceFile": "15-doc-09-custom-instructions-memory.md", "title": "Document 9: Custom Instructions and Memory: Setup Guide", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack.**\n**Applies to:** ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), Gemini (Google), Copilot (Microsoft), and local tools (LM Studio, Ollama).\n**Version:** October 2026. Menus move; if a step below does not match your screen, look for the nearest label in Settings containing \"personalisation\", \"memory\", \"profile\" or \"context\".\n\n---\n\n## In plain terms\n\nWhen an AI tool \"remembers\" you, it has simply saved some notes about you as ordinary text and re-reads them at the start of each chat. You can open those notes, fix them and delete them, and you should, once a month. The instructions you write yourself are the ones worth getting right.\n\n## First, what \"memory\" actually is\n\nPicture the receptionist at your local café. They keep a sticky note about regular customers: \"Sam, flat white, no sugar, runs the plumbing business\". Before Sam walks in, they glance at the note. They haven't learned anything deeper about Sam; they've just got a note.\n\nThat's what AI \"memory\" is. The product stores ordinary text about you: either facts it picked out of your conversations (\"runs a plumbing business in Geelong; prefers short emails\") or notes you wrote yourself. It quietly places that text in front of your future conversations, in the same spot as the system prompt from Class 4. That's all.\n\n**What it isn't:** the model's numbers aren't changing as you chat. Nothing is \"getting to know you\" the way a person does. Memory is a saved note that gets re-read, which means you can read it, edit it and delete it like any other text. You should, because out-of-date notes (\"I'm preparing the March launch\") quietly skew future answers.\n\n**Two different features hide behind the word:**\n- **Custom instructions:** text you write on purpose, applied every time. The laminated card from Class 4. Set this up carefully.\n- **Memory:** text the product writes for itself from your conversations, applied automatically. The receptionist's sticky notes. Supervise these.\n\n---\n\n## ChatGPT (OpenAI)\n\n**Custom instructions (yours, on purpose).** On the web: click your profile name or icon (bottom left), then Settings, Personalisation, Custom instructions. You'll see two boxes:\n- Box one, \"What would you like ChatGPT to know about you or your work?\": who you are and your context (business, role, location, the systems you use).\n- Box two, \"How would you like ChatGPT to respond?\": your standing rules. Paste the matching Document 1 template here.\n\nSave. On mobile: menu icon, your name at the top, then Personalisation and Custom instructions, with the same two boxes.\n\n**Memory (it writes, you supervise).** Settings, Personalisation, Memory. Two things live here:\n- \"Saved memories\": facts it decided to keep. Each one is a line of text with a bin icon beside it.\n- A \"Reference chat history\" style switch that lets it draw on past conversations.\n\nTo review, open Memory settings and read the list. You'll see plain sentences, which proves the point about what memory is. To delete, use the bin icon on each one, or \"Clear all memories\". To stop new ones forming, switch the memory settings off. To save something on purpose, say in a chat \"Remember that our quotes are valid for 30 days\", then check it appears in the list.\n\n**Projects.** Sidebar, New project. Each project has its own instructions box and file area, and depending on settings its chats can be kept out of your account's general memory. Best practice for work: one project per recurring area (Quotes, Marketing, Admin), instructions pasted from Document 1, and standing reference files added as Document 2 describes.\n\n**Consumer-tier warning:** on Free or Plus, also check Settings, Data controls, and turn off model training. On Business or Enterprise plans, memory and training are controlled by your organisation's admin and the contract.\n\n## Claude (Anthropic)\n\n**Profile / personal preferences (yours, on purpose).** Web or app: your initial or name (top corner), Settings, Profile. In the field asking what personal preferences Claude should consider, paste your identity lines and rules (Document 1 template). This applies across your chats.\n\n**Projects (the workhorse).** Sidebar, New project. Open the project, then its settings. You'll find a custom instructions field (paste the full Document 1 template for that context) and a Project knowledge area, where uploaded files stay available to every chat in the project. Claude's Projects are the cleanest \"separate filing drawer for each part of the business\" setup among the major products: one project per type of client, one per recurring task.\n\n**Memory.** Claude offers memory features on some plans: it can build summaries of past conversations and carry context forward. Look under Settings for Memory, or in a chat's tools menu. The supervision rules are the same: open it, read what it stored as plain text, delete what's out of date.\n\nOn consumer plans, the training and retention choices from Document 5 affect how long anything is kept: about 30 days by default, and up to five years if you opted in to training, so don't opt in at work. On Team or Enterprise plans, memory is controlled by your organisation's admin and your content isn't used for training.\n\n## Gemini (Google)\n\n**Personal context (yours, on purpose).** gemini.google.com or the app: Settings (gear icon), then look for Personal context or \"What should Gemini know about you\" (Google has also called this Saved info). Paste a shortened version of your template. The field rewards brevity: who you are, plus your five most important rules.\n\n**Gems (standing assistants).** Sidebar, Gems (or gemini.google.com/gems), New Gem. Name it (\"[Business] assistant\") and paste the full Document 1 template into its instructions. Every conversation started inside that Gem follows the template. Create one Gem per major area of work. This is Google's equivalent of Projects and, for business use, the better half of Gemini. Workspace users: depending on plan, Gems and personal context are also available inside Gmail, Docs and Meet.\n\n**Memory / activity.** Gemini's ability to draw on your past chats and your Google apps is controlled by Gemini Apps Activity (Settings, then the Activity page).\n- **On:** prompts are saved to your Google Account, can shape personalisation and, as Document 5 explains, may feed product improvement.\n- **Off:** chats aren't saved to your account history.\n\nReview what's stored on the Activity page, and delete items one at a time or in bulk there. For work, the recommended setting is off, unless your Workspace admin has set business defaults, in which case Workspace protections apply and the consumer concerns mostly don't.\n\n**NotebookLM (bonus, and excellent).** notebooklm.google.com: create a notebook, upload your sources (documents, PDFs, links), and every answer is based strictly on those sources, with references you can click to check. It's an open-book exam the tool can't cheat on. For \"answer only from my documents\" work (policies, contracts, manuals) it's the strongest easily available tool in Google's range, and for business use it sits under your Workspace account.\n\n## Copilot (Microsoft)\n\n**Consumer Copilot (personal Microsoft account).** In the Copilot app or copilot.microsoft.com: Settings, Personalisation, and enter your preferences. There's also a memory-style feature that stores facts from your chats, which you can review and delete in the same settings area, plus conversation history management in the Microsoft privacy dashboard (privacy.microsoft.com). As Document 5 says, check the product-improvement and data-sharing switches here too.\n\n**Microsoft 365 Copilot (work account).** Personal settings are limited on purpose.\n- **The standing briefing** is set for the whole organisation by your IT admin (organisational prompts and custom agents, through the Microsoft 365 admin centre and Copilot Studio).\n- **\"Memory\" of your work** comes from something else entirely: Copilot can see the mail, files, chats and calendar you can see, within the permissions already in place.\n\nWhat to do as an employee: send your Document 1 template to your admin and ask whether it can be applied for everyone. Day to day, you can rely on the fact that with a work account, your prompts aren't used to train the underlying models. Checking \"what it knows\" in this world means checking your actual Microsoft 365 permissions with your admin, which is a good idea regardless of AI.\n\n## Local tools (Ollama, LM Studio)\n\nThere's no vendor memory because there's no vendor. It remembers whatever you set up.\n\n- **LM Studio:** the settings panel on the right of the chat screen has a System prompt box. Fill it with your Document 1 template, then save it as a preset so every new chat loads it. Chat history is stored on your own computer, and you can delete it like any file.\n- **Ollama:** system prompts are set per session, in a settings file called a Modelfile, or through whatever front-end app you use.\n\nAnything beyond that is your own habit: keep your reference documents in a folder, paste or attach what you need each session, and your computer stays the only place any of it exists. That's the whole privacy appeal in one sentence.\n\n---\n\n## The maintenance routine (put it in your calendar)\n\n**Monthly, five minutes:** open the memory or review screen of each tool you use (ChatGPT: Settings, Personalisation, Memory. Claude: Settings, Memory/Profile. Gemini: the Activity page. Copilot: personalisation settings) and delete anything out of date or wrong. Read it for what it is: sticky notes written by a very literal intern.\n\n**Quarterly, with your Document 5 and 6 review:**\n- re-read your custom instructions against how your business actually works now, and update the templates;\n- check that an app update hasn't switched the training and retention settings back on;\n- on shared or admin-managed tools, confirm the organisation's settings still match your policy (Document 3).\n\n**One rule above all:** on consumer tiers, memory features store notes about you on the vendor's computers. Anything you wouldn't want on that note (a health detail you mentioned once, a client's name, a money worry) shouldn't be said to a consumer tool in the first place. The delete button is a mop; the decision drill is the tap."}, {"slug": "doc-10-incident-response-card", "sourceFile": "16-doc-10-incident-response-card.md", "title": "Document 10: Incident Response Quick Card", "subtitle": "", "md": "**Part of the \"AI Without the Sales Pitch\" participant pack. One page. Print it, pin it where the coffee machine is.**\n**Business:** [name]. **Policy owner / first call:** [name, mobile]. **Second call:** [name]. **Incident log:** [location].\n**Version:** October 2026. Calm, procedural, no drama. Same-day reporting is always the right move.\n\n---\n\n## In plain terms\n\nThis is the evacuation map for AI mistakes: read it once now, so on the bad day you follow steps instead of panicking. Every scenario has the same spine: stop, tell the policy owner the same day, fix what you can, be honest with anyone affected, and write one line in the log.\n\n---\n\n## Scenario 1: An employee realises they pasted sensitive data into an AI tool\n\n*(Client details, financials, health information, passwords, anything under NDA.)*\n\n1. **Stop using that chat, and write down the facts:** which tool, which account (personal or work), roughly what data, what time. Two minutes.\n2. **Tell the policy owner the same day.** There's no penalty for reporting on the day. Delay is the only thing that turns a log entry into an incident.\n3. **Delete the conversation** in the tool. On consumer tools, also check the training and opt-out settings (Document 5) and note whether they were on. If the vendor offers a data-deletion request (most have one in privacy settings or through support), the policy owner submits it.\n4. **If a password, access key or login was pasted: change it immediately,** before anything else on this list. Assume it's been seen. Don't wait for the assessment.\n5. **Assess (policy owner, same day):**\n   - Was it personal information covered by the Privacy Act?\n   - Could it cause serious harm to anyone?\n   - Was the tool a free consumer account (worse) or a contracted business account (contained by its data processing agreement)?\n\n   Most cases end here: \"logged, contained, a quick word about the decision drill\".\n6. **If serious harm is possible:** treat it as a potential notifiable data breach. The policy owner, with legal advice if needed, checks it against the Notifiable Data Breaches scheme. If it qualifies, notify the privacy regulator (the OAIC) and the affected people. Fast, factual notice does far less damage than a late discovery.\n7. **Log it** in one line (date, tool, type of data, what was done, outcome) and fix the pattern, not the person. The fix is usually approved tools, the decision drill, or one toolbox talk.\n\n## Scenario 2: A client asks whether AI was used in their deliverable\n\n1. **Answer honestly, specifically and without embarrassment.** Model sentence: \"We use AI tools for drafting and summarising, on contracted business platforms where our data isn't used to train their models. A qualified person on our team checked every figure and claim before this reached you. Here is our policy.\" Attach or offer Document 3.\n2. **Check the contract before saying anything more.** If the engagement restricts AI use, the policy owner compares what was done with what was promised, and we raise any gap ourselves. Staying quiet and hoping isn't a strategy; Deloitte's refund is the case study.\n3. **Never claim checks that didn't happen.** If nobody checked the work, say what was actually done. A smaller honest claim survives. A large false one ends relationships and can breach consumer law.\n4. **Log the question** (date, client, answer given). Repeated questions from clients are market research: they tell you a disclosure paragraph belongs in your standard engagement letter. Ask your lawyer to add one.\n5. **Feed it back.** If the question exposed a gap (a tool that isn't on the approved list, an unchecked draft that went out), run the relevant checklist here and update the policy.\n\n## Scenario 3: A data breach involving an AI vendor is reported in the news\n\n1. **Get the facts before forming an opinion:** which vendor, which product and tier, what type of data, what time period, and is it confirmed or rumour? Check the vendor's security and status pages first, the news second.\n2. **Check whether we're affected:** does anyone here use that product? Which tier are we on (consumer or contracted business)? Did our use during the affected period involve personal or sensitive information? The tool register (Class 6) makes this a ten-minute job, which is exactly why the register exists.\n3. **If we're affected and on a business tier:** the vendor's obligations under its data processing agreement kick in. The policy owner writes to the vendor's support or security team and asks directly: was our data involved, what was exposed, what are you doing about it, and will you notify us formally? Keep the email thread.\n4. **Work out our own duty to notify:** under the Notifiable Data Breaches scheme, a breach at our vendor can still be our responsibility to report. If personal information we control was involved and serious harm is likely, get advice promptly, and notify the OAIC and the people affected. Early, plain-language notice to affected clients beats them reading about it in the news.\n5. **Take protective steps regardless:** where you can, change any passwords or access keys that were used with that vendor; review what we send them from now on; consider pausing sensitive use until their report is out.\n6. **If we're not affected:** log the check anyway (one line), and use it as a reminder to confirm the register is up to date. Next time, you'll be glad it exists.\n\n## Scenario 4: AI-produced output was wrong and it went to a customer\n\n*(A wrong price, a fake deadline, an invented policy, a made-up reference in something that was sent, published or filed.)*\n\n1. **Contain it.** If the wrong output is still live (website, portal, scheduled posts), take it down now. If it was sent, note exactly who received it and when.\n2. **Correct it with the customer, promptly and plainly.** Model: \"An error in the [quote/summary/report] we sent on [date]: [the specific error]. The correct position is [fact]. We have corrected the attached version.\" Own the error as the business; don't blame the software to the customer. Nobody has ever been reassured by \"our AI said so\". Air Canada tried that argument before a tribunal and lost.\n3. **Assess the damage honestly.** Did anyone act on the wrong information: pay, sign, commit, rely on it? If money or commitments are involved, bring in the policy owner, and if the amount or risk is significant, your lawyer or insurer. The same week, not the same quarter.\n4. **Find the hole in the process, not a villain.** Ask:\n   - Was the real material given to the tool? If not, it filled the gap by inventing, the bluffing student from Class 1.\n   - Did the human check happen? (Document 4, Pattern B; Document 7, Steps 3 and 5.)\n   - Was this a type of output our policy says needs a second pair of eyes?\n\n   The cause is nearly always a skipped step, and skipped steps are fixed with a checklist, not a lecture.\n5. **Fix it and log it.** Add the specific check to the workflow, run a five-minute toolbox talk if it could happen elsewhere, and log the incident (date, what went out, the correction, the process fix). Two or three logged incidents of the same shape tell you exactly which workflow needs redesigning. That log is one of the most useful documents a small business can keep.\n\n---\n\n**All four scenarios share one rule: speed beats perfection.** Telling someone the same day, correcting in plain language and following up in writing sorts out almost everything at small scale. What makes small problems big is delay, hiding, and improvising answers under pressure. That's why this card exists, and why the first call on it is a person, not a search engine."}], "extras": [{"slug": "facilitator-notes", "sourceFile": "17-facilitator-notes.md", "title": "Facilitator Notes", "subtitle": "", "md": "**AI Without the Sales Pitch. For whoever delivers the classes.**\n\n---\n\n## How this course teaches: the Feynman Technique\n\nThe physicist Richard Feynman's test was simple: if you can't explain something simply, you don't understand it yet. Every class is built on that test, and your delivery should be too.\n\n**The four moves, in every class:**\n\n1. **Picture first, word second.** Open each idea with an everyday picture the room already knows, then name the technical term and translate it. Never the other way round. \"The model has a desk, and the size of the desk is called the context window\" lands. \"The context window is the maximum token count\" doesn't.\n2. **One picture per idea, used consistently.** The learners read the same pictures in their chapters, so use the same ones out loud. Switching analogies mid-course costs people the thread.\n3. **Say where the picture breaks.** Analogies mislead if you stretch them. Each chapter has a \"where the picture breaks\" note; mention it. It builds trust, and it stops the sharp person in the second row from derailing you.\n4. **Make them explain it back.** Understanding shows up when people try to explain. The \"Explain it back\" box at the end of each chapter is the debrief: pairs, three sentences each, no jargon allowed.\n\n**The picture kit** (keep these consistent across all six classes):\n\n| Idea | The picture |\n|---|---|\n| What a model is | Phone autocomplete at enormous scale; a sound desk with billions of locked sliders |\n| Hallucination | The student bluffing an exam essay: knows what a good answer sounds like, not what's true |\n| Tokens | The meter running: like kilowatt-hours on a power bill |\n| Context window | The model's desk; when it overflows, the oldest papers fall off the edge |\n| Model versus product | The engine and the car |\n| Cloud versus local | The hire car versus the ute you own and service yourself |\n| Your data's journey | Posting a letter to a company overseas that keeps a copy on file |\n| Training on your data | A teacher's habits shaped by marking essays, not a filing cabinet anyone can open |\n| Data processing agreement | The key register: who holds which key, which doors, when they hand it back |\n| Prompting | Briefing a temp on day one: the job sheet (role, task, material, constraints) |\n| System prompt | The laminated card on the temp's desk |\n| Grounding | An open-book exam |\n| Marketing claims | Real estate listings: \"cosy\" means small |\n| Demo versus pilot | The staged open home versus the building inspection |\n| Build tiers | Light bulb, garden shed, wiring: DIY versus a licensed electrician |\n| Shadow AI | A staff member's own ute on a work delivery, uninsured |\n| The four habits | Food safety in a commercial kitchen |\n| Policy owner | The fire warden |\n| Incident card | The evacuation map by the lift |\n\n## Suggested pacing (per class, 90 minutes with a break, or trim to 60)\n\n- **0:00 to 0:10:** welcome, a show of hands (\"who has used one of these tools this month?\"), and the promise of the series. Never open with definitions; open with their lives. Read \"The short version\" aloud as the promise of the night.\n- **0:10 to 0:45:** the first half of the chapter, as talk plus a live screen. Show, don't describe: have the tool open and do one real thing while you make each point.\n- **0:45 to 0:55:** break. Talk to people. The best material for later classes comes from break-time questions.\n- **0:55 to 1:10:** the second half of the chapter, finishing with \"Myth, meet reality\". Deliver the myth last; it's the memory hook.\n- **1:10 to 1:25:** the practical exercise. Circulate. Work in pairs, not solo; people on their own disengage at keyboards.\n- **1:25 to 1:30:** \"Explain it back\" in pairs: each person explains the night's main idea to their partner in three sentences, no jargon, and the partner points out any word that needed translating. Then a one-line takeaway, homework (install the relevant document from the pack this week), and a teaser for next class.\n\n**Class-specific timing notes:** Class 3's settings safari needs everyone logged in, so send a \"bring your device and your login\" reminder 48 hours ahead. Class 5's demo must be rehearsed twice with a pre-built fallback file, because live AI builds fail on stage with cruel regularity, and the failure is only funny the first time.\n\n## Common questions to expect (and the honest one-liner)\n\n- **\"Which one is best?\"** \"Best at what? The software you already use decides more than any test score. Document 6, the two-question method.\"\n- **\"Is it free though? What's the catch?\"** \"The catch is your data on consumer accounts. That's Class 3.\"\n- **\"Will it take my job?\"** Document 8, question 1, out loud, no flinching. Audiences respect a straight answer here more than anywhere else.\n- **\"My nephew says the new model is conscious / AGI is next year.\"** Class 1: a stack of numbers doing pattern-matching, the bluffing student. \"Conscious\" is a metaphor doing unpaid work.\n- **\"Can I trust it for my tax return?\"** It can explain, draft and organise; a registered agent lodges. Fluent isn't the same as checked.\n- **\"What does it cost to set all this up properly?\"** The Class 6 rollout: a couple of business seats, one page of policy, one week. Under A$150 a month for a ten-person business.\n- **\"Someone in my office is already pasting client stuff into ChatGPT.\"** That's the amnesty conversation in Class 6. Hand them Document 3 tonight, not next month.\n\n## Handling \"but I heard that...\" objections\n\nThe pattern is always the same: a headline, a podcast clip or a brother-in-law, remembered with more confidence than detail. Four moves, in order:\n\n1. **Ask for the source and date.** \"Where did you hear that, and roughly when?\" Half of objections dissolve here, because the claim is three model generations old or came from someone with something to sell. This is Class 2's field test, applied gently to the objector.\n2. **Separate what it can do today from what someone predicts.** \"AI can write a whole app\" may be true today, for some kinds of app, with supervision. \"AI will write all software by next year\" is a future-tense marketing sentence. Concede the first; examine the second.\n3. **Agree with the grain of truth.** Every lasting myth grew around something real. \"AI makes stuff up\" is true, and it's the reason for our whole checking habit. \"AI will change work\" is true, and it's Class 5. Agreeing with the grain of truth earns you room for the correction.\n4. **Offer a test, not an argument.** \"Bring the claim next week and we'll run it live on your own material.\" Nothing converts a sceptic like watching their own document get summarised well, or their own doomsday claim fail a pilot. Never win an argument with a person in front of a room; win them a demonstration.\n\n**Specific recurring ones:**\n- **\"I heard ChatGPT is being sued for copyright.\"** True and unresolved, and it's about how the vendors trained their models, not about whether you may use the output for your quote emails. Watch this space, and buy business tiers with protective terms if it matters to you.\n- **\"I heard these tools use enormous amounts of water and power.\"** True at industry scale, and a legitimate reason for the hybrid approach and for not asking a datacentre to do what your laptop can.\n- **\"My competitor says AI cut their costs 40%.\"** Class 2, word for word: whose measure, checked how? Klarna is the standing case study.\n\n## Room craft\n\n- Demo with the free tiers most attendees actually have, then mention what the paid tier changes. Never demo enterprise features nobody in the room will ever touch.\n- Keep printed copies of Documents 4 and 10 on every table. People take paper.\n- When someone shares a horror story, thank them and use it. The horror stories are the curriculum; every one of them maps to a checklist in the pack.\n- If you catch yourself using a technical word before its picture, stop and back up. The room will forgive the restart; it won't forgive being lost.\n- Watch the clock in Class 5. The build exercise always runs long, because debugging is public and debugging is slow. Have the fallback ready.\n- Local government attendees will ask about procurement and record-keeping obligations. Point them to Class 6's vendor questions plus their own records officer. Don't improvise legal answers.\n\n## Legal line for facilitators\n\nSay this once per class: \"Everything in this course is practical guidance, not legal advice. For your specific situation, especially health information or contracts, talk to a lawyer or privacy professional.\" Then keep teaching. The disclaimer protects you; it doesn't excuse vagueness."}, {"slug": "research-notes-and-sources", "sourceFile": "18-research-notes-and-sources.md", "title": "Research Notes and Sources", "subtitle": "", "md": "**AI Without the Sales Pitch. What was verified, when, and where it came from.**\n**Research date: 13 October 2026.** This file underpins Documents 5 and 6 and Classes 2 and 3. Re-verify quarterly.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Pricing and plan structures (verified 13 October 2026, web research)\n\n**ChatGPT (OpenAI).** OpenAI's pricing page confirms Free plus paid plans (Go, Plus, Business, Enterprise) priced per user per month. Third-party trackers (CloudZero, Sep 2026; alloypress, Jul 2026; dancumberlandlabs, Oct 2026; coworker.ai, Jul 2026) converge on: Go ~US$8/mo (select markets), Plus US$20/mo, Pro US$100–200/mo by usage tier, Business (formerly Team) ~US$25–30/user/mo, Enterprise quote-only with procurement reports converging on US$45–75/seat/mo with seat minimums. Note: \"Team\" was renamed \"Business\"; older articles still say Team.\n\n**Claude (Anthropic).** claude.com/pricing (accessed via search): Free; Pro US$17/mo annual or US$20 monthly; Max from US$100/mo (US$200 higher tier); Team ~US$25/seat/mo annual (5-seat minimum typical) with a Premium tier (~US$150/seat) adding Claude Code allowances; Enterprise quote-only. A 2026 Anthropic solutions page references a US$20/seat annual structure with a 20-seat minimum and retired plan names, confirming churn in naming; always quote the live pricing page.\n\n**Gemini / Google Workspace.** Google's Workspace pricing and knowledge-base pages confirm Gemini features are bundled into Workspace plans (the separate \"Gemini for Workspace\" add-on era ended in 2025). Third-party (eesel.ai, Jun 2026): Business Starter ~US$7, Standard ~US$14, Plus ~US$22–26/user/mo; Enterprise quote-only. Consumer: free Gemini app; Google AI Pro ~US$19.99/mo; AI Ultra US$249.99/mo (Google's own AI plans page).\n\n**Microsoft Copilot.** microsoft.com/copilot/pricing/business (current): lists US$32/user/mo annual for a \"Premium with Copilot\" tier; third-party trackers (gosearch.ai, Sep 2026) report Copilot Free US$0, Copilot Pro ~US$20/mo, Microsoft 365 Copilot Business ~US$21/user/mo annual commitment (up to 300 users), Enterprise ~US$30/user/mo annual with qualifying M365 plan, and Copilot Chat included free with M365 business subscriptions (metered agents extra). Microsoft's support pages confirm the free consumer Copilot at copilot.cloud.microsoft and the distinction from paid Microsoft 365 Copilot. Because Microsoft's own pages show multiple figures for similarly named offers, Document 6 carries a range and a verify warning.\n\n**Meta Llama (local).** Open weights under the Meta Community Licence, free for businesses below the hyperscale usage threshold; no per-seat cost; hardware is the cost. See local AI section below.\n\n## 2. Data-handling policies (verified 13 October 2026)\n\n- **OpenAI:** enterprise-privacy page (Jan 2026) states business data is not used for training by default. Multiple privacy trackers (text.com Jun 2026; sonomos.ai May 2026; strac.io) confirm: consumer Free/Plus trained on by default unless opted out (Settings, Data controls); Pro excluded; Business/Enterprise excluded by default with DPAs.\n- **Anthropic:** \"Updates to Consumer Terms and Privacy Policy\" (anthropic.com, Aug 2025) confirms training on consumer data moved to an opt-in with a consent prompt, and opting in extends retention from ~30 days to up to 5 years. Anthropic privacy centre (2026) confirms the 30-day default and 5-year opt-in retention; Team/Enterprise excluded from training.\n- **Google:** Workspace knowledge base confirms Workspace prompt data protections (not trained on outside the domain; 18-month default retention for certain Gemini app data). Ars Technica (Apr 2026) documents the consumer settings maze: training/product improvement on consumer prompts requires Gemini Apps Activity to be on; users must find and disable it themselves.\n- **Microsoft:** learn.microsoft.com (Aug 2026) confirms Microsoft 365 Copilot prompts/responses/Graph data are not used to train foundation LLMs and are covered by the Microsoft DPA (enterprise data protection) when signed in with a work account; consumer Copilot chats may be used for product improvement unless opted out (meprism.com opt-out guide, Aug 2026; whathumansask.com, Jul 2026, summarising the consumer/work split).\n\n## 3. Marketing versus reality: the named cases used in Class 2\n\n- **Deloitte Australia / Department of Home Affairs report (October 2025):** Deloitte agreed to partially refund a ~$290,000 portion (report total reported ~$440,000) after reviewers (notably Dr Christopher Rudge) found apparent AI-generated errors including fabricated references and quotations; Deloitte admitted AI use contrary to contractual terms. Sources: Fortune (7 Oct 2025), CFO Dive (14 Oct 2025), US News, AI Incident Database (#1193).\n- **Klarna:** early-2024 claim that its AI assistant did the work of 700 customer-service agents; May 2025 public reversal by CEO Sebastian Siemiatkowski, resuming human hiring for quality. Forrester's \"AI washing\" framing (attributing financially motivated cuts to technology) is cited via reporting referenced in search results.\n- **Air Canada chatbot:** BC Civil Resolution Tribunal decision (Feb 2024) held the airline liable for its chatbot's wrong bereavement-fare information; the \"separate legal entity\" defence failed. (Pre-dates the 12-month window; used as a foundational case with its date stated.)\n- **SEC \"AI washing\" enforcement (March 2024):** Delphia fined US$225,000 and Global Predictions US$175,000 for false claims about AI use. (Foundational case, dated as such; FTC/ACCC/ASIC scrutiny of AI claims continues per klover.ai enterprise analysis, Sep 2026.)\n- **McDonald's IBM drive-through AI trial ended (June 2024)** after viral order hallucinations. (Foundational case, dated.)\n- **\"PhD-level\"/AGI-timeline claims** (OpenAI and peers, 2025–2026) used as the live future-tense example, framed as roadmap language rather than today's capability.\n\nWhere cases pre-date the \"last 12 months\" window they are explicitly dated in the class copy, because the teaching value outweighs recency and the class promises honesty about dates.\n\n## 4. Local AI state of play (2025–2026)\n\n- Hardware rules of thumb (sitepoint.com definitive guide, Feb 2026; pinggy.io, Jun 2026; iproyal.com, Aug 2026; localllm.in): ~7–9B open models usable on 16GB machines including Apple Silicon unified memory; 12–32B models want 24–32GB memory or a 12GB+ VRAM GPU; CPU-only Windows laptops run small models slowly but workably.\n- Tooling: Ollama and LM Studio remain the accessible entry points (install, select model, chat); vLLM/Jan noted for advanced users. Open-weight families cited: Meta Llama (3.1/4 generation), Qwen, Mistral, Gemma.\n- Limitations as taught: capability gap versus frontier cloud models, no native ecosystem integrations, one-person maintenance burden, sharing across an office requires server setup.\n\n## 5. Australian Privacy Act implications\n\n- **Privacy and Other Legislation Amendment Act 2024 (first tranche):** staged commencement; stronger OAIC powers, higher civil penalties, children's online privacy code process (OAIC APP Guidelines update confirmed via OAIC LinkedIn, 2025).\n- **Statutory tort for serious invasions of privacy:** in force since 10 June 2025; no turnover test.\n- **Automated decision-making transparency (APP 1):** from **10 December 2026**, privacy policies must describe, in general terms, significant automated decision-making including AI-shaped decisions (OAIC consultation on ADM guidance, May 2026; xcdit.com.au and responsibleaiaustralia.com.au compliance notes, 2026).\n- **APP 8 (cross-border disclosure)** and **APP 11 (security)** applied to chatbot use as taught in Class 3; **Notifiable Data Breaches scheme** applied to vendor breaches in Document 10.\n- **Small-business exemption:** still present; removal flagged in future reform tranches (second tranche not yet legislated as at research date). Course advice: do not build a strategy on the exemption.\n- **OAIC generative AI guidance:** minimise, secure, be transparent, keep humans accountable; cited as the plainest official statement of \"reasonable steps.\"\n- All Privacy Act content in the package is framed as practical guidance with an explicit not-legal-advice line (facilitator notes).\n\n## 6. Currency warning\n\nPricing figures, plan names (OpenAI's Team→Business rename; Anthropic's tier-name churn; Microsoft's multiple similarly priced offers) and settings paths in Documents 5, 6 and 9 will drift. Both time-sensitive documents carry \"last verified: 13 October 2026\" headers and quarterly review instructions. The conceptual content (Classes 1 to 6, Documents 1 to 4, 7, 8, 10) is deliberately built on mechanics that change slowly."}]};
